{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789885028581,"sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","part":{"id":"prt_0bd7618e1001DYuf0rLYdXXlfM","messageID":"msg_0bd7615e8001kQtEA8dcYgK6iV","sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885031844,"sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1c7654451be84c71a6a67503","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\.opencode\\skills\\interactive-comprehensive-exam-generator\\SKILL.md","offset":1987},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\.opencode\\skills\\interactive-comprehensive-exam-generator\\SKILL.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1987: ## 99. Professional Visual Design\n1988: \n1989: The examination system MUST have a professional, polished academic/exam-platform appearance.\n1990: \n1991: The design should communicate:\n1992: \n1993: - Serious examination purpose\n1994: - Clear information hierarchy\n1995: - Easy reading\n1996: - Consistent spacing\n1997: - Consistent typography\n1998: - Strong visual grouping\n1999: - Clear interactive states\n2000: - Responsive behavior\n2001: \n2002: Use a restrained, professional visual system rather than decorative effects.\n2003: \n2004: Avoid:\n2005: \n2006: - Excessive gradients\n2007: - Excessive animations\n2008: - Unnecessary glassmorphism\n2009: - Distracting illustrations\n2010: - Oversized decorative elements\n2011: - Cluttered dashboards\n2012: - Inconsistent colors\n2013: - Excessive rounded cards\n2014: - Visual effects that reduce readability\n2015: \n2016: The interface should feel like a modern professional assessment platform rather than a casual game.\n2017: \n2018: Use visual hierarchy for:\n2019: \n2020: ```text\n2021: Application Header\n2022:     ↓\n2023: Navigation / Chapter Tabs\n2024:     ↓\n2025: Exam Title + Description\n2026:     ↓\n2027: Mode Selection\n2028:     ↓\n2029: Question Area\n2030:     ↓\n2031: Answer Controls\n2032:     ↓\n2033: Feedback / Submission\n2034:     ↓\n2035: Progress / Results\n2036: ```\n2037: \n2038: Maintain consistent styling across:\n2039: \n2040: - Buttons\n2041: - Tabs\n2042: - Cards\n2043: - Inputs\n2044: - Radio buttons\n2045: - Checkboxes\n2046: - Text fields\n2047: - Timers\n2048: - Progress indicators\n2049: - Feedback messages\n2050: - Results screens\n2051: \n2052: All states should have visually distinct but accessible:\n2053: \n2054: - Default\n2055: - Hover\n2056: - Focus\n2057: - Selected\n2058: - Correct\n2059: - Incorrect\n2060: - Disabled\n2061: - Submitted\n2062: - Locked\n2063: \n2064: Do not communicate correctness through color alone. Pair color with text, icons, borders, or other accessible indicators.\n2065: \n2066: ---\n2067: \n2068: ## 100. Exam Experience Modes\n2069: \n2070: The system must support TWO independent exam experience modes.\n2071: \n2072: These modes describe HOW the exam is taken, not whether it is timed.\n2073: \n2074: ### Mode A — Interactive / One-by-One\n2075: \n2076: This mode should behave like a flashcard or Quizizz-style examination.\n2077: \n2078: Display only one primary question at a time.\n2079: \n2080: Flow:\n2081: \n2082: ```text\n2083: Question\n2084:    ↓\n2085: Student answers\n2086:    ↓\n2087: Submit / Select Answer\n2088:    ↓\n2089: Immediate validation\n2090:    ↓\n2091: Correct / Incorrect feedback\n2092:    ↓\n2093: Correct answer + concise explanation\n2094:    ↓\n2095: Question becomes locked\n2096:    ↓\n2097: Next Question\n2098: ```\n2099: \n2100: Requirements:\n2101: \n2102: - Show one question at a time.\n2103: - Validate the answer immediately.\n2104: - Immediately display whether the answer is correct or incorrect.\n2105: - Reveal the correct answer after submission.\n2106: - Show a concise explanation when available.\n2107: - Update score immediately.\n2108: - Lock the submitted question.\n2109: - Prevent changing an already submitted answer.\n2110: - Provide a clear `Next` control.\n2111: - Show current question number and total.\n2112: - Show progress.\n2113: - Show score where appropriate.\n2114: \n2115: The experience should feel sequential:\n2116: \n2117: ```text\n2118: Q1 → Answer → Feedback → Next\n2119: Q2 → Answer → Feedback → Next\n2120: Q3 → Answer → Feedback → Next\n2121: ```\n2122: \n2123: Do not show the entire chapter as a long form in this mode.\n2124: \n2125: ### Mode B — Form / One-Time Submit\n2126: \n2127: This mode should behave like Google Forms.\n2128: \n2129: Display multiple questions vertically in a scrollable form.\n2130: \n2131: Requirements:\n2132: \n2133: - Show the questions together.\n2134: - Allow the student to answer questions without immediate correctness feedback.\n2135: - Do not reveal answers while the form is being completed.\n2136: - Provide one final `Submit Exam` action.\n2137: - Validate the entire exam only after submission.\n2138: - Calculate the final score after submission.\n2139: - Show results after submission.\n2140: - Optionally show per-question corrections after submission.\n2141: - Lock the submitted attempt unless the user explicitly resets/restarts it.\n2142: \n2143: The student should experience:\n2144: \n2145: ```text\n2146: Question 1\n2147: Question 2\n2148: Question 3\n2149: ...\n2150: Question N\n2151:         ↓\n2152: Submit Exam\n2153:         ↓\n2154: Validate All\n2155:         ↓\n2156: Results\n2157: ```\n2158: \n2159: Do not accidentally make this mode behave like Interactive mode.\n2160: \n2161: ---\n2162: \n2163: ## 101. Experience Mode Independence\n2164: \n2165: Experience mode and time mode are separate settings.\n2166: \n2167: The system must support combinations such as:\n2168: \n2169: ```text\n2170: Interactive + Timed\n2171: Interactive + Untimed\n2172: \n2173: Form + Timed\n2174: Form + Untimed\n2175: ```\n2176: \n2177: If the implementation does not support a particular combination for a specific exam type, the UI must clearly disable that combination rather than pretending it works.\n2178: \n2179: Do not confuse:\n2180: \n2181: ```text\n2182: Experience Mode:\n2183: Interactive / Form\n2184: \n2185: Time Mode:\n2186: Timed / Untimed\n2187: ```\n2188: \n2189: They must have independent state.\n2190: \n2191: Example:\n2192: \n2193: ```javascript\n2194: examSettings = {\n2195:     experienceMode: \"interactive\",\n2196:     timeMode: \"untimed\"\n2197: };\n2198: ```\n2199: \n2200: A Timed exam can still be Interactive or Form-like.\n2201: \n2202: An Untimed exam can still be Interactive or Form-like.\n2203: \n2204: ---\n2205: \n2206: ## 102. Existing Timed and Untimed Modes\n2207: \n2208: Timed and Untimed modes ALREADY EXIST in this specification.\n2209: \n2210: Preserve all requirements from Sections 5–8.\n2211: \n2212: Timed:\n2213: \n2214: ```text\n2215: 10 seconds per question\n2216: ```\n2217: \n2218: Untimed:\n2219: \n2220: ```text\n2221: No countdown\n2222: No timeout\n2223: Unlimited answering time\n2224: ```\n2225: \n2226: Do not replace the existing Timed/Untimed system with the new Interactive/Form modes.\n2227: \n2228: Instead, combine them as separate dimensions:\n2229: \n2230: ```text\n2231: Chapter\n2232:  ├── Experience Mode\n2233:  │    ├── Interactive\n2234:  │    └── Form\n2235:  │\n2236:  └── Time Mode\n2237:       ├── Timed\n2238:       └── Untimed\n2239: ```\n2240: \n2241: ---\n2242: \n2243: ## 103. Chapter Exam-Type Selection\n2244: \n2245: Each chapter must support different examination types.\n2246: \n2247: Supported types:\n2248: \n2249: ```text\n2250: Multiple Choice\n2251: Identification\n2252: True or False\n2253: Enumeration\n2254: ```\n2255: \n2256: A chapter may use one type or a deliberate mixture of types.\n2257: \n2258: Do not force every chapter to use the same exam type.\n2259: \n2260: Example:\n2261: \n2262: ```text\n2263: Chapter 1 → Multiple Choice\n2264: Chapter 2 → Identification\n2265: Chapter 3 → True or False\n2266: Chapter 4 → Enumeration\n2267: ```\n2268: \n2269: Another valid configuration:\n2270: \n2271: ```text\n2272: Chapter 1\n2273:     Multiple Choice\n2274:     Identification\n2275:     True or False\n2276: \n2277: Chapter 2\n2278:     Multiple Choice\n2279:     Enumeration\n2280: ```\n2281: \n2282: The selected exam type must be clearly shown before the exam begins.\n2283: \n2284: ---\n2285: \n2286: ## 104. Multiple Choice\n2287: \n2288: Multiple Choice questions must follow the existing requirements in Sections 13–45.\n2289: \n2290: Requirements:\n2291: \n2292: - Exactly four choices\n2293: - A, B, C, D\n2294: - Exactly one correct answer\n2295: - Plausible distractors\n2296: - Balanced answer positions\n2297: - No answer-pattern clues\n2298: - Immediate validation in Interactive mode\n2299: - Batch validation in Form mode\n2300: \n2301: Do not apply four-choice requirements to Identification, True/False, or Enumeration questions.\n2302: \n2303: ---\n2304: \n2305: ## 105. Identification Questions\n2306: \n2307: Identification questions require the student to provide the answer rather than select from choices.\n2308: \n2309: Example:\n2310: \n2311: ```text\n2312: Question:\n2313: What command displays the current working directory?\n2314: \n2315: Answer:\n2316: pwd\n2317: ```\n2318: \n2319: Requirements:\n2320: \n2321: - Use a text input.\n2322: - Do not show answer choices.\n2323: - Validate according to the reference.\n2324: - Ignore irrelevant capitalization when appropriate.\n2325: - Normalize harmless surrounding whitespace.\n2326: - Do not accept obviously different concepts merely because they are vaguely related.\n2327: - Support documented alternate answers when the reference clearly permits them.\n2328: \n2329: Example normalization:\n2330: \n2331: ```text\n2332: \" PWD \"\n2333: \"pwd\"\n2334: \"Pwd\"\n2335: ```\n2336: \n2337: may be treated equivalently when capitalization is not conceptually meaningful.\n2338: \n2339: For answers where capitalization matters according to the reference, preserve the distinction.\n2340: \n2341: Identification validation should support an explicit answer structure:\n2342: \n2343: ```javascript\n2344: {\n2345:     type: \"identification\",\n2346:     acceptedAnswers: [\"pwd\"],\n2347:     explanation: \"...\"\n2348: }\n2349: ```\n2350: \n2351: If multiple equivalent answers are valid:\n2352: \n2353: ```javascript\n2354: {\n2355:     type: \"identification\",\n2356:     acceptedAnswers: [\n2357:         \"answer one\",\n2358:         \"answer 1\"\n2359:     ]\n2360: }\n2361: ```\n2362: \n2363: Do not invent alternate answers.\n2364: \n2365: ---\n2366: \n2367: ## 106. Identification Feedback\n2368: \n2369: Interactive Identification:\n2370: \n2371: ```text\n2372: Student enters answer\n2373:         ↓\n2374: Submit\n2375:         ↓\n2376: Validate\n2377:         ↓\n2378: Correct / Incorrect\n2379:         ↓\n2380: Show accepted answer\n2381:         ↓\n2382: Explanation\n2383:         ↓\n2384: Next\n2385: ```\n2386: \n2387: Form Identification:\n2388: \n2389: ```text\n2390: Student enters all answers\n2391:         ↓\n2392: Submit Exam\n2393:         ↓\n2394: Validate all answers\n2395:         ↓\n2396: Show results\n2397: ```\n2398: \n2399: Do not reveal the correct answer before submission.\n2400: \n2401: ---\n2402: \n2403: ## 107. True or False Questions\n2404: \n2405: True or False questions must contain exactly two possible answers:\n2406: \n2407: ```text\n2408: True\n2409: False\n2410: ```\n2411: \n2412: Requirements:\n2413: \n2414: - Statement must be supported by the reference.\n2415: - Only one answer may be correct.\n2416: - Avoid trivial wording.\n2417: - Avoid unsupported absolute statements.\n2418: - Avoid ambiguity.\n2419: - Clearly identify the statement being judged.\n2420: - Test actual understanding rather than guessing from wording.\n2421: \n2422: Example:\n2423: \n2424: ```text\n2425: [ True ] [ False ]\n2426: \n2427: The reference states that ______.\n2428: ```\n2429: \n2430: The correct answer must be explicitly supported by the source material.\n2431: \n2432: ---\n2433: \n2434: ## 108. Enumeration Questions\n2435: \n2436: Enumeration questions require the student to provide multiple items.\n2437: \n2438: Example:\n2439: \n2440: ```text\n2441: Question:\n2442: List the three categories identified in the reference.\n2443: \n2444: Answer:\n2445: 1. ______\n2446: 2. ______\n2447: 3. ______\n2448: ```\n2449: \n2450: Requirements:\n2451: \n2452: - The required number of items must be explicit when the reference provides a fixed number.\n2453: - Define the expected answer items in structured data.\n2454: - Validate each required item.\n2455: - Ignore harmless capitalization and surrounding whitespace when appropriate.\n2456: - Do not require a specific order unless the reference establishes that order as meaningful.\n2457: - If order is meaningful, preserve and validate order.\n2458: - Do not invent additional acceptable items.\n2459: \n2460: Example:\n2461: \n2462: ```javascript\n2463: {\n2464:     type: \"enumeration\",\n2465:     requiredItems: [\n2466:         \"item one\",\n2467:         \"item two\",\n2468:         \"item three\"\n2469:     ],\n2470:     orderMatters: false\n2471: }\n2472: ```\n2473: \n2474: For order-sensitive material:\n2475: \n2476: ```javascript\n2477: {\n2478:     type: \"enumeration\",\n2479:     requiredItems: [\n2480:         \"step one\",\n2481:         \"step two\",\n2482:         \"step three\"\n2483:     ],\n2484:     orderMatters: true\n2485: }\n2486: ```\n2487: \n2488: ---\n2489: \n2490: ## 109. Enumeration Scoring\n2491: \n2492: Enumeration scoring must be explicit and consistent.\n2493: \n2494: For example, if five items are required:\n2495: \n2496: ```text\n2497: 5/5 → Full credit\n2498: 4/5 → Partial credit\n2499: 3/5 → Partial credit\n2500: ...\n2501: ```\n2502: \n2503: If the exam uses all-or-nothing scoring, state that clearly in the interface or exam configuration.\n2504: \n2505: Do not silently switch scoring behavior between chapters.\n2506: \n2507: Example:\n2508: \n2509: ```javascript\n2510: scoring: {\n2511:     type: \"partial\",\n2512:     pointsPerItem: 1\n2513: }\n2514: ```\n2515: \n2516: or:\n2517: \n2518: ```javascript\n2519: scoring: {\n2520:     type: \"allOrNothing\"\n2521: }\n2522: ```\n2523: \n2524: The chosen scoring model must be consistent for that exam.\n2525: \n2526: ---\n2527: \n2528: ## 110. Exam-Type Metadata\n2529: \n2530: Every question should identify its question type.\n2531: \n2532: Example:\n2533: \n2534: ```javascript\n2535: {\n2536:     id: \"ch1-q01\",\n2537:     chapter: 1,\n2538:     type: \"multipleChoice\"\n2539: }\n2540: ```\n2541: \n2542: Supported values:\n2543: \n2544: ```text\n2545: multipleChoice\n2546: identification\n2547: trueFalse\n2548: enumeration\n2549: ```\n2550: \n2551: Additional metadata may include:\n2552: \n2553: ```javascript\n2554: {\n2555:     id: \"ch1-q02\",\n2556:     chapter: 1,\n2557:     type: \"identification\",\n2558:     topic: \"Topic Name\",\n2559:     difficulty: \"hard\",\n2560:     bloomLevel: \"apply\"\n2561: }\n2562: ```\n2563: \n2564: The UI must render the correct answer control based on `type`.\n2565: \n2566: ---\n2567: \n2568: ## 111. Question-Type Diversity\n2569: \n2570: When a chapter uses multiple exam types, distribute them deliberately.\n2571: \n2572: Avoid generating an arbitrary mixture.\n2573: \n2574: A reasonable example:\n2575: \n2576: ```text\n2577: 30-question chapter\n2578: \n2579: Multiple Choice   → 15\n2580: Identification    → 7\n2581: True or False     → 5\n2582: Enumeration       → 3\n2583: ```\n2584: \n2585: The exact distribution may change depending on the reference.\n2586: \n2587: Prioritize:\n2588: \n2589: 1. Reference coverage\n2590: 2. Assessment quality\n2591: 3. Suitability of question type\n2592: 4. Difficulty\n2593: 5. Variety\n2594: \n2595: Do not force a question into a type that does not fit the material.\n2596: \n2597: ---\n2598: \n2599: ## 112. Dedicated Identification Tab\n2600: \n2601: In addition to the normal chapter tabs, provide a dedicated:\n2602: \n2603: ```text\n2604: Identification\n2605: ```\n2606: \n2607: tab.\n2608: \n2609: This tab is specifically for identification examinations across chapters.\n2610: \n2611: Navigation should resemble:\n2612: \n2613: ```text\n2614: Chapter 1\n2615: Chapter 2\n2616: Chapter 3\n2617: Chapter 4\n2618: Identification\n2619: Overall Exam\n2620: Answer Key\n2621: Encyclopedia\n2622: ```\n2623: \n2624: The Identification tab must NOT simply duplicate one chapter's normal exam.\n2625: \n2626: It should provide access to identification questions from every chapter.\n2627: \n2628: Example:\n2629: \n2630: ```text\n2631: Identification\n2632: \n2633: Chapter 1\n2634: [Start Identification Exam]\n2635: \n2636: Chapter 2\n2637: [Start Identification Exam]\n2638: \n2639: Chapter 3\n2640: [Start Identification Exam]\n2641: \n2642: Chapter 4\n2643: [Start Identification Exam]\n2644: ```\n2645: \n2646: ---\n2647: \n2648: ## 113. Identification Tab Behavior\n2649: \n2650: The dedicated Identification tab must:\n2651: \n2652: - Show available chapters.\n2653: - Show the number of identification questions for each chapter.\n2654: - Allow the user to start an identification exam for a selected chapter.\n2655: - Use text-input answers.\n2656: - Support Interactive and Form experience modes when configured.\n2657: - Support Timed and Untimed modes when configured.\n2658: - Validate answers according to Identification rules.\n2659: - Display results after completion.\n2660: - Preserve the original chapter identification question bank.\n2661: - Not alter the normal chapter exam state.\n2662: \n2663: Example:\n2664: \n2665: ```text\n2666: Identification\n2667: ────────────────────────────\n2668: \n2669: Chapter 1\n2670: 8 questions\n2671: [Start]\n2672: \n2673: Chapter 2\n2674: 6 questions\n2675: [Start]\n2676: \n2677: Chapter 3\n2678: 10 questions\n2679: [Start]\n2680: ```\n2681: \n2682: The dedicated Identification tab is a separate examination entry point.\n2683: \n2684: ---\n2685: \n2686: ## 114. Identification State Independence\n2687: \n2688: Identification exams must maintain independent state.\n2689: \n2690: Do not reuse:\n2691: \n2692: ```text\n2693: chapter multiple-choice score\n2694: chapter currentQuestion\n2695: chapter selectedAnswer\n2696: chapter timer\n2697: chapter completion state\n2698: ```\n2699: \n2700: unless the implementation explicitly creates a separate Identification attempt state.\n2701: \n2702: Example:\n2703: \n2704: ```javascript\n2705: identificationExamState = {\n2706:     chapter: 1,\n2707:     experienceMode: \"interactive\",\n2708:     timeMode: \"untimed\",\n2709:     currentQuestion: 0,\n2710:     score: 0,\n2711:     completed: false\n2712: };\n2713: ```\n2714: \n2715: A completed Multiple Choice exam must not automatically mark its Identification exam as completed.\n2716: \n2717: ---\n2718: \n2719: ## 115. Navigation and Active Exam Locking\n2720: \n2721: The existing chapter-locking rules remain active for every exam type and experience mode.\n2722: \n2723: While an exam is active, prevent:\n2724: \n2725: - Switching to another chapter\n2726: - Opening another exam\n2727: - Opening the Overall Exam\n2728: - Opening the Answer Key\n2729: - Opening answer-revealing reference material\n2730: - Changing experience mode\n2731: - Changing time mode\n2732: \n2733: This applies to:\n2734: \n2735: ```text\n2736: Multiple Choice\n2737: Identification\n2738: True or False\n2739: Enumeration\n2740: ```\n2741: \n2742: and:\n2743: \n2744: ```text\n2745: Interactive\n2746: Form\n2747: ```\n2748: \n2749: After completion or reset, normal navigation becomes available.\n2750: \n2751: ---\n2752: \n2753: ## 116. Mode Selection Screen\n2754: \n2755: Before starting a chapter exam, display a professional configuration screen.\n2756: \n2757: Example:\n2758: \n2759: ```text\n2760: Chapter 1\n2761: Database Fundamentals\n2762: \n2763: Exam Type\n2764: [ Multiple Choice ]\n2765: \n2766: Experience\n2767: (●) Interactive\n2768: (○) Form\n2769: \n2770: Time\n2771: (●) Untimed\n2772: (○) Timed — 10 seconds/question\n2773: \n2774: Questions\n2775: 30\n2776: \n2777: [ Start Exam ]\n2778: ```\n2779: \n2780: For an exam that supports multiple question types:\n2781: \n2782: ```text\n2783: Exam Type\n2784: (●) Mixed\n2785: (○) Multiple Choice\n2786: (○) Identification\n2787: (○) True or False\n2788: (○) Enumeration\n2789: ```\n2790: \n2791: Only display configuration options that are actually supported by that exam.\n2792: \n2793: ---\n2794: \n2795: ## 117. Mixed Exam Mode\n2796: \n2797: If Mixed exam type is enabled, questions may contain:\n2798: \n2799: ```text\n2800: Multiple Choice\n2801: Identification\n2802: True or False\n2803: Enumeration\n2804: ```\n2805: \n2806: The question interface must automatically change according to the active question type.\n2807: \n2808: Example:\n2809: \n2810: ```text\n2811: Q1 → Multiple Choice\n2812:     [A] [B] [C] [D]\n2813: \n2814: Q2 → Identification\n2815:     [Text Input]\n2816: \n2817: Q3 → True or False\n2818:     [True] [False]\n2819: \n2820: Q4 → Enumeration\n2821:     [Item 1]\n2822:     [Item 2]\n2823:     [Item 3]\n2824: ```\n2825: \n2826: Do not display irrelevant controls.\n2827: \n2828: ---\n2829: \n2830: ## 118. Form Mode Submission Rules by Question Type\n2831: \n2832: In Form mode, all supported question types must be submitted together.\n2833: \n2834: Example:\n2835: \n2836: ```text\n2837: Question 1 — Multiple Choice\n2838: ○ A\n2839: ○ B\n2840: ○ C\n2841: ○ D\n2842: \n2843: Question 2 — Identification\n2844: [____________]\n2845: \n2846: Question 3 — True or False\n2847: ○ True\n2848: ○ False\n2849: \n2850: Question 4 — Enumeration\n2851: 1. [__________]\n2852: 2. [__________]\n2853: 3. [__________]\n2854: \n2855: [Submit Exam]\n2856: ```\n2857: \n2858: No question should receive correctness feedback before the final submission.\n2859: \n2860: After submission:\n2861: \n2862: - Lock all inputs.\n2863: - Calculate results.\n2864: - Show score.\n2865: - Show question-level correctness.\n2866: - Show correct answers where appropriate.\n2867: - Show explanations.\n2868: - Provide Review/Reset controls.\n2869: \n2870: ---\n2871: \n2872: ## 119. Interactive Mode Submission Rules by Question Type\n2873: \n2874: Interactive mode must validate each question independently.\n2875: \n2876: Multiple Choice:\n2877: \n2878: ```text\n2879: Select choice\n2880: → Immediate validation\n2881: ```\n2882: \n2883: Identification:\n2884: \n2885: ```text\n2886: Enter answer\n2887: → Submit\n2888: → Immediate validation\n2889: ```\n2890: \n2891: True or False:\n2892: \n2893: ```text\n2894: Select True/False\n2895: → Immediate validation\n2896: ```\n2897: \n2898: Enumeration:\n2899: \n2900: ```text\n2901: Enter required items\n2902: → Submit\n2903: → Immediate validation\n2904: ```\n2905: \n2906: After validation, lock the current question and allow the user to continue.\n2907: \n2908: ---\n2909: \n2910: ## 120. Results by Exam Type\n2911: \n2912: Results should clearly report performance appropriate to the question types used.\n2913: \n2914: At minimum:\n2915: \n2916: ```text\n2917: Final Score\n2918: Correct\n2919: Incorrect\n2920: Unanswered\n2921: Percentage\n2922: ```\n2923: \n2924: For mixed or partial-credit exams, also show:\n2925: \n2926: ```text\n2927: Points Earned\n2928: Points Possible\n2929: ```\n2930: \n2931: For Identification and Enumeration, provide useful review information without exposing answers before submission.\n2932: \n2933: ---\n2934: \n2935: ## 121. Professional Empty and Error States\n2936: \n2937: The interface must handle unusual states professionally.\n2938: \n2939: Examples:\n2940: \n2941: ```text\n2942: No identification questions available for this chapter.\n2943: ```\n2944: \n2945: ```text\n2946: This exam type is not available for this chapter.\n2947: ```\n2948: \n2949: ```text\n2950: Please answer all required items before submitting.\n2951: ```\n2952: \n2953: ```text\n2954: No questions were generated because the reference does not support this exam type.\n2955: ```\n2956: \n2957: Do not show blank screens, undefined values, broken controls, or fake question counts.\n2958: \n2959: ---\n2960: \n2961: ## 122. Updated Navigation Requirement\n2962: \n2963: The complete navigation should support:\n2964: \n2965: ```text\n2966: Chapter Tabs\n2967: ├── Chapter 1\n2968: ├── Chapter 2\n2969: ├── Chapter 3\n2970: └── ...\n2971: \n2972: Dedicated Exam Tabs\n2973: ├── Identification\n2974: └── Overall Exam\n2975: \n2976: Utility Tabs\n2977: ├── Answer Key\n2978: └── Encyclopedia\n2979: ```\n2980: \n2981: If additional exam-type tabs are implemented later, they must have a clear purpose and must not duplicate existing functionality unnecessarily.\n2982: \n2983: ---\n2984: \n2985: ## 123. Updated Final Delivery Checklist\n2986: \n2987: Add the following requirements to the existing checklist:\n2988: \n2989: ```text\n2990: [ ] Professional academic/exam-platform visual design\n2991: [ ] Responsive professional layout\n2992: [ ] Interactive / One-by-One experience mode works\n2993: [ ] Form / Google Forms-like experience mode works\n2994: [ ] Interactive mode validates immediately\n2995: [ ] Interactive mode locks answered questions\n2996: [ ] Form mode validates only after final submission\n2997: [ ] Form mode locks after submission\n2998: [ ] Experience mode and Time mode are independent\n2999: [ ] Timed + Interactive works\n3000: [ ] Untimed + Interactive works\n3001: [ ] Timed + Form works when supported\n3002: [ ] Untimed + Form works\n3003: [ ] Multiple Choice supported\n3004: [ ] Identification supported\n3005: [ ] True or False supported\n3006: [ ] Enumeration supported\n3007: [ ] Question type metadata is correct\n3008: [ ] Identification validation is normalized and reference-grounded\n3009: [ ] Enumeration scoring is explicitly defined\n3010: [ ] Mixed exam type works when enabled\n3011: [ ] Dedicated Identification tab exists\n3012: [ ] Identification tab contains exams for each chapter\n3013: [ ] Identification state is independent\n3014: [ ] Navigation locking applies to all exam types\n3015: [ ] Mode-selection screen clearly separates Experience Mode from Time Mode\n3016: [ ] Results work for all supported question types\n3017: [ ] Empty/error states are handled professionally\n3018: ```\n3019: \n3020: ---\n3021: \n3022: ## 124. Final Experience Requirement\n3023: \n3024: The completed system should provide two distinct ways to study and assess each chapter:\n3025: \n3026: ### Interactive Study/Exam\n3027: \n3028: ```text\n3029: Choose Chapter\n3030:       ↓\n3031: Choose Exam Type\n3032:       ↓\n3033: Choose Interactive\n3034:       ↓\n3035: Choose Timed / Untimed\n3036:       ↓\n3037: Start\n3038:       ↓\n3039: One question\n3040:       ↓\n3041: Answer\n3042:       ↓\n3043: Immediate validation\n3044:       ↓\n3045: Feedback\n3046:       ↓\n3047: Next question\n3048: ```\n3049: \n3050: ### Form-Style Exam\n3051: \n3052: ```text\n3053: Choose Chapter\n3054:       ↓\n3055: Choose Exam Type\n3056:       ↓\n3057: Choose Form\n3058:       ↓\n3059: Choose Timed / Untimed\n3060:       ↓\n3061: Start\n3062:       ↓\n3063: Complete full form\n3064:       ↓\n3065: Submit once\n3066:       ↓\n3067: Validate entire exam\n3068:       ↓\n3069: Results\n3070: ```\n3071: \n3072: The system must make these experiences visibly and behaviorally different.\n3073: \n3074: The final product should feel professional enough for serious academic review while remaining simple enough for a student to understand immediately.\n3075: \n3076: The goal is not merely to generate questions. The goal is to provide a complete, reference-grounded assessment platform with multiple examination types, multiple interaction modes, independent timed/untimed behavior, chapter-specific assessments, and a dedicated identification examination area.\n3077: \n3078: \n3079: ## 125. Intelligent Exam Generation — Core Requirement\n3080: \n3081: The exam generator MUST NOT select questions using simple random sampling alone. Randomness may be used only after the generator has determined that the selected set satisfies the required learning coverage and quality constraints.\n3082: \n3083: The generator's primary goal is useful assessment, not merely variety.\n3084: \n3085: For every generated exam, the generator MUST first analyze the available reference material and build a question pool containing:\n3086: \n3087: - source section/chapter\n3088: - topic/concept\n3089: - learning target or fact being tested\n3090: - question type\n3091: - difficulty\n3092: - cognitive demand\n3093: - importance/centrality of the concept\n3094: - whether the concept has already been tested\n3095: - acceptable answer(s), when applicable\n3096: - source/reference location\n3097: - potential distractor quality, for Multiple Choice\n3098: \n3099: If the source material does not contain enough information to support a requested question, the generator MUST NOT invent one merely to reach a target question count.\n3100: \n3101: ## 126. Exam Blueprint Before Question Selection\n3102: \n3103: Before generating questions, create an internal exam blueprint.\n3104: \n3105: The blueprint MUST determine:\n3106: \n3107: 1. which major topics must be represented;\n3108: 2. how many questions each topic should receive;\n3109: 3. which important concepts require direct assessment;\n3110: 4. which concepts can be assessed through application or comparison;\n3111: 5. which question types are appropriate for each concept;\n3112: 6. the intended difficulty distribution;\n3113: 7. the intended cognitive-demand distribution;\n3114: 8. which concepts should not be repeated unnecessarily;\n3115: 9. which questions should be reserved for other modes/exams;\n3116: 10. the final number of questions.\n3117: \n3118: The generator should prefer broad and meaningful coverage over repeatedly asking about the same easy facts.\n3119: \n3120: Example:\n3121: \n3122: ```text\n3123: Chapter coverage\n3124: ├── Topic A — 20%\n3125: ├── Topic B — 25%\n3126: ├── Topic C — 30%\n3127: └── Topic D — 25%\n3128: \n3129: Difficulty\n3130: ├── Easy — 30%\n3131: ├── Medium — 50%\n3132: └── Hard — 20%\n3133: \n3134: Cognitive demand\n3135: ├── Recall — 30%\n3136: ├── Understanding — 40%\n3137: └── Application/Analysis — 30%\n3138: ```\n3139: \n3140: These percentages are defaults, not mandatory values. The generator MUST adapt them to the actual source material.\n3141: \n3142: ## 127. Concept Coverage and Anti-Redundancy\n3143: \n3144: The generator MUST track concepts rather than treating every question as independent.\n3145: \n3146: Each question should contain metadata similar to:\n3147: \n3148: ```js\n3149: {\n3150:   topic: \"permissions\",\n3151:   concept: \"chmod numeric permissions\",\n3152:   sourceSection: \"File Permissions\",\n3153:   type: \"multipleChoice\",\n3154:   difficulty: \"medium\",\n3155:   cognitiveLevel: \"application\",\n3156:   importance: \"high\"\n3157: }\n3158: ```\n3159: \n3160: The generator MUST avoid producing several questions that test the same fact using only superficial wording changes.\n3161: \n3162: For example, these should count as substantially the same concept:\n3163: \n3164: ```text\n3165: What does chmod 755 do?\n3166: What permissions does 755 provide?\n3167: Which permission mode is equivalent to rwxr-xr-x?\n3168: ```\n3169: \n3170: Only one or a limited number should normally appear in the same exam unless repetition is intentionally required for mastery.\n3171: \n3172: ## 128. Intelligent Generation for Interactive Mode\n3173: \n3174: Interactive / One-by-One mode MUST use questions that work well with immediate feedback.\n3175: \n3176: The generator should prioritize:\n3177: \n3178: - clear, focused questions;\n3179: - one concept at a time;\n3180: - questions that can be confidently validated immediately;\n3181: - meaningful distractors for Multiple Choice;\n3182: - concise expected answers for Identification;\n3183: - unambiguous True/False statements;\n3184: - manageable Enumeration prompts.\n3185: \n3186: Interactive mode SHOULD progressively vary the cognitive demand rather than presenting a sequence of trivial recall questions.\n3187: \n3188: A typical generated sequence should intentionally mix:\n3189: \n3190: 1. foundational recall;\n3191: 2. understanding;\n3192: 3. application;\n3193: 4. comparison or interpretation;\n3194: 5. targeted reinforcement of weak areas when the system supports adaptive behavior.\n3195: \n3196: The generator MUST NOT simply shuffle the same fixed question bank and call that adaptive.\n3197: \n3198: ## 129. Intelligent Generation for Form / One-Time Submit Mode\n3199: \n3200: Form / One-Time Submit mode MUST be designed as a coherent complete assessment.\n3201: \n3202: The generator should prioritize:\n3203: \n3204: - comprehensive topic coverage;\n3205: - balanced difficulty;\n3206: - low redundancy;\n3207: - logical ordering;\n3208: - a mixture of question types when configured;\n3209: - enough questions to measure the chapter without unnecessary repetition.\n3210: \n3211: The form should normally progress from foundational questions toward more demanding questions, unless the configured exam blueprint specifies another order.\n3212: \n3213: The generator MUST avoid placing several nearly identical questions next to one another.\n3214: \n3215: ## 130. Intelligent Generation for Timed Mode\n3216: \n3217: Timed mode MUST account for the limited time available.\n3218: \n3219: Question selection MUST consider:\n3220: \n3221: - expected reading time;\n3222: - expected answer time;\n3223: - question complexity;\n3224: - enumeration length;\n3225: - identification ambiguity;\n3226: - number of choices;\n3227: - cognitive demand.\n3228: \n3229: Timed exams SHOULD avoid requiring unusually long responses when the time limit is short.\n3230: \n3231: For the existing default of 10 seconds per question, the generator should strongly favor questions that can reasonably be read, understood, and answered within that constraint.\n3232: \n3233: Long Enumeration and complex Identification prompts SHOULD be excluded from a 10-second Timed exam unless the reference material and configuration clearly justify them.\n3234: \n3235: The generator MUST NOT create a Timed exam by taking an Untimed exam and simply adding a timer.\n3236: \n3237: ## 131. Intelligent Generation for Untimed Mode\n3238: \n3239: Untimed mode can support deeper assessment.\n3240: \n3241: The generator may include:\n3242: \n3243: - more complex application questions;\n3244: - comparison questions;\n3245: - longer Identification prompts when supported by the material;\n3246: - Enumeration requiring several source-supported items;\n3247: - questions requiring careful interpretation.\n3248: \n3249: However, Untimed MUST still prioritize useful coverage and must not become a collection of unnecessarily difficult questions.\n3250: \n3251: ## 132. Intelligent Generation by Experience × Time Combination\n3252: \n3253: Experience Mode and Time Mode are independent, but question generation MUST account for their combination.\n3254: \n3255: The generator MUST evaluate these four combinations separately:\n3256: \n3257: ```text\n3258: Interactive + Timed\n3259: Interactive + Untimed\n3260: Form + Timed\n3261: Form + Untimed\n3262: ```\n3263: \n3264: Each combination may use a different generated question set from the same source material.\n3265: \n3266: The system MUST NOT assume that one universal randomized question list is appropriate for all four combinations.\n3267: \n3268: ## 133. Intelligent Generation by Question Type\n3269: \n3270: Question type selection MUST be concept-driven.\n3271: \n3272: ### Multiple Choice\n3273: \n3274: Use Multiple Choice when:\n3275: \n3276: - the source supports clear alternatives;\n3277: - distractors can be constructed from plausible misunderstandings or related concepts;\n3278: - exactly one answer can be defended from the source.\n3279: \n3280: Distractors MUST be plausible but clearly incorrect according to the reference material.\n3281: \n3282: Do not create distractors that are obviously absurd merely to fill four choices.\n3283: \n3284: ### Identification\n3285: \n3286: Use Identification when the target is a:\n3287: \n3288: - term;\n3289: - command;\n3290: - concept;\n3291: - definition;\n3292: - named item;\n3293: - clearly identifiable fact.\n3294: \n3295: Identification should NOT be used when multiple answers could reasonably satisfy the prompt unless all accepted answers are explicitly defined.\n3296: \n3297: ### True or False\n3298: \n3299: Use True/False for precise statements where the source clearly supports either True or False.\n3300: \n3301: Avoid statements that depend on:\n3302: \n3303: - vague wording;\n3304: - hidden exceptions;\n3305: - unsupported assumptions;\n3306: - subjective interpretation.\n3307: \n3308: ### Enumeration\n3309: \n3310: Use Enumeration when the reference explicitly provides:\n3311: \n3312: - a list;\n3313: - several required steps;\n3314: - multiple components;\n3315: - categories;\n3316: - options/items that should be recalled together.\n3317: \n3318: The generator MUST NOT invent additional enumeration items.\n3319: \n3320: ## 134. Cross-Type Coverage\n3321: \n3322: When a chapter supports multiple question types, the generator should test the same important concept from different useful angles only when doing so adds assessment value.\n3323: \n3324: Example:\n3325: \n3326: ```text\n3327: Concept: chmod numeric permissions\n3328: \n3329: Identification:\n3330: \"What command changes file permissions?\"\n3331: \n3332: Multiple Choice:\n3333: \"Which mode represents rwxr-xr-x?\"\n3334: \n3335: True/False:\n3336: \"chmod 755 gives the owner read, write, and execute permissions.\"\n3337: \n3338: Enumeration:\n3339: \"List the permission values represented by r, w, and x.\"\n3340: ```\n3341: \n3342: These should NOT all automatically appear in the same exam.\n3343: \n3344: The generator should select the representation that best measures the intended learning target and use another representation only when it tests a distinct skill.\n3345: \n3346: ## 135. Difficulty Generation\n3347: \n3348: Difficulty MUST be determined from the source-supported reasoning required, not from arbitrary wording.\n3349: \n3350: Suggested criteria:\n3351: \n3352: ### Easy\n3353: - direct recall;\n3354: - explicit definition;\n3355: - directly stated fact;\n3356: - straightforward recognition.\n3357: \n3358: ### Medium\n3359: - interpretation;\n3360: - comparison;\n3361: - applying a documented rule;\n3362: - selecting between plausible alternatives.\n3363: \n3364: ### Hard\n3365: - multi-step application;\n3366: - combining multiple source-supported concepts;\n3367: - interpreting a non-obvious but defensible scenario;\n3368: - distinguishing closely related concepts.\n3369: \n3370: A question MUST NOT be labeled Hard merely because it is obscure or intentionally confusing.\n3371: \n3372: ## 136. Cognitive-Demand Balance\n3373: \n3374: The generator should track cognitive demand separately from difficulty.\n3375: \n3376: Possible levels:\n3377: \n3378: ```text\n3379: Recall\n3380: Understanding\n3381: Application\n3382: Analysis\n3383: ```\n3384: \n3385: The generator should avoid making an entire exam Recall-only when the source material supports deeper questions.\n3386: \n3387: However, it MUST NOT force Application or Analysis questions when the reference material does not provide enough information to support them.\n3388: \n3389: ## 137. Learning-Value Scoring for Candidate Questions\n3390: \n3391: Every candidate question should receive an internal quality/utility assessment before selection.\n3392: \n3393: Suggested factors:\n3394: \n3395: ```text\n3396: coverageValue\n3397: importanceValue\n3398: cognitiveValue\n3399: difficultyFit\n3400: questionTypeFit\n3401: sourceConfidence\n3402: redundancyPenalty\n3403: ambiguityPenalty\n3404: timeCost\n3405: ```\n3406: \n3407: A conceptual selection score may be calculated as:\n3408: \n3409: ```text\n3410: utility =\n3411:   coverageValue\n3412:   + importanceValue\n3413:   + cognitiveValue\n3414:   + difficultyFit\n3415:   + questionTypeFit\n3416:   + sourceConfidence\n3417:   - redundancyPenalty\n3418:   - ambiguityPenalty\n3419:   - timeCostPenalty\n3420: ```\n3421: \n3422: The exact numeric weights are implementation details. The important requirement is that question selection MUST consider these factors rather than selecting candidates uniformly at random.\n3423: \n3424: ## 138. Randomness as a Final Tie-Breaker\n3425: \n3426: Random selection MAY be used only after candidate questions have passed quality and blueprint constraints.\n3427: \n3428: Correct sequence:\n3429: \n3430: ```text\n3431: Source Material\n3432:       ↓\n3433: Extract Concepts\n3434:       ↓\n3435: Build Question Candidates\n3436:       ↓\n3437: Validate Candidates\n3438:       ↓\n3439: Build Exam Blueprint\n3440:       ↓\n3441: Filter Low-Value / Redundant Questions\n3442:       ↓\n3443: Match Coverage + Difficulty + Cognitive Demand\n3444:       ↓\n3445: Select High-Utility Questions\n3446:       ↓\n3447: Use Randomness Only Among Comparable Candidates\n3448:       ↓\n3449: Run Final Validation\n3450:       ↓\n3451: Generate Exam\n3452: ```\n3453: \n3454: Incorrect sequence:\n3455: \n3456: ```text\n3457: Question Bank\n3458:       ↓\n3459: Random.shuffle()\n3460:       ↓\n3461: Take first N questions\n3462: ```\n3463: \n3464: The second approach MUST NOT be used as the primary exam-generation strategy.\n3465: \n3466: ## 139. Adaptive Reinforcement Without Random Noise\n3467: \n3468: If the system tracks user performance, future Interactive exams MAY use previous results to reinforce weak concepts.\n3469: \n3470: For example:\n3471: \n3472: ```text\n3473: Previous result:\n3474: Permissions = weak\n3475: Archives = strong\n3476: Navigation = strong\n3477: ```\n3478: \n3479: The next Interactive exam may increase coverage of Permissions while still retaining broader chapter coverage.\n3480: \n3481: The system MUST NOT turn this into repetitive drilling of one question.\n3482: \n3483: Adaptive selection should target:\n3484: \n3485: - weak concepts;\n3486: - missed question types;\n3487: - misunderstood distinctions;\n3488: - underrepresented high-importance topics.\n3489: \n3490: It should still maintain balanced coverage.\n3491: \n3492: ## 140. Per-Mode Question Bank Separation\n3493: \n3494: The generator should maintain logical pools:\n3495: \n3496: ```text\n3497: chapterBank\n3498: ├── interactiveBank\n3499: ├── formBank\n3500: ├── timedBank\n3501: ├── untimedBank\n3502: ├── identificationBank\n3503: ├── multipleChoiceBank\n3504: ├── trueFalseBank\n3505: └── enumerationBank\n3506: ```\n3507: \n3508: These are logical filtered views and do not necessarily require duplicated physical questions.\n3509: \n3510: A question may belong to multiple compatible pools, but each exam generation pass MUST apply the rules for the selected mode.\n3511: \n3512: ## 141. Exam Generation Audit\n3513: \n3514: Before displaying an exam, automatically audit the generated set.\n3515: \n3516: The audit MUST check:\n3517: \n3518: - requested question count;\n3519: - chapter/topic coverage;\n3520: - major concept coverage;\n3521: - question-type distribution;\n3522: - difficulty distribution;\n3523: - cognitive-demand distribution;\n3524: - duplicate concepts;\n3525: - near-duplicate wording;\n3526: - answer uniqueness;\n3527: - source support;\n3528: - distractor quality;\n3529: - Timed feasibility;\n3530: - Identification accepted answers;\n3531: - Enumeration scoring;\n3532: - True/False ambiguity;\n3533: - no unsupported facts.\n3534: \n3535: If the audit fails, regenerate or replace only the problematic questions.\n3536: \n3537: Do NOT blindly regenerate the entire exam with a new random seed, because that can reintroduce the same quality problems.\n3538: \n3539: ## 142. Regeneration Strategy\n3540: \n3541: When an exam fails validation:\n3542: \n3543: 1. identify the exact failed constraint;\n3544: 2. identify the question(s) responsible;\n3545: 3. remove only invalid or low-utility candidates;\n3546: 4. select replacement candidates that improve the failed constraint;\n3547: 5. rerun the audit;\n3548: 6. repeat until valid or until the source material is exhausted.\n3549: \n3550: If the source cannot satisfy a requested blueprint, the system should report the limitation rather than inventing content.\n3551: \n3552: ## 143. Exam-to-Exam Variation\n3553: \n3554: Different attempts MAY vary question selection, but variation must preserve educational coverage.\n3555: \n3556: For example:\n3557: \n3558: ```text\n3559: Attempt 1:\n3560: Topic A → Q1\n3561: Topic B → Q4\n3562: Topic C → Q7\n3563: \n3564: Attempt 2:\n3565: Topic A → Q3\n3566: Topic B → Q8\n3567: Topic C → Q10\n3568: ```\n3569: \n3570: Both attempts should still assess the important concepts.\n3571: \n3572: The generator MUST NOT interpret \"different exam\" as \"completely different random questions with no coverage guarantees.\"\n3573: \n3574: ## 144. Chapter-Level Blueprint Inheritance\n3575: \n3576: Each chapter should first receive its own blueprint based on the actual content of that chapter.\n3577: \n3578: The Overall Exam should then construct a higher-level blueprint from all chapters.\n3579: \n3580: The Overall Exam MUST NOT simply concatenate random questions from each chapter.\n3581: \n3582: It should consider:\n3583: \n3584: - chapter representation;\n3585: - overall topic coverage;\n3586: - duplicate concepts across chapters;\n3587: - total assessment length;\n3588: - question-type balance;\n3589: - difficulty progression;\n3590: - form-style vertical presentation requirements.\n3591: \n3592: ## 145. Identification-Only Tab Generation\n3593: \n3594: The dedicated Identification tab MUST use the same intelligent generation principles.\n3595: \n3596: It should NOT simply extract random questions and convert them into Identification format.\n3597: \n3598: For each chapter:\n3599: \n3600: 1. identify concepts suitable for Identification;\n3601: 2. verify that the concept has a clear expected answer;\n3602: 3. define accepted answer variants where justified;\n3603: 4. remove ambiguous concepts;\n3604: 5. balance coverage across important terms;\n3605: 6. generate the Identification exam;\n3606: 7. validate answers against the source.\n3607: \n3608: ## 146. Mode-Specific Generation Examples\n3609: \n3610: The implementation should conceptually support results such as:\n3611: \n3612: ```text\n3613: Interactive + Untimed\n3614: → deeper application and immediate reinforcement\n3615: \n3616: Interactive + Timed\n3617: → concise, quickly answerable questions\n3618: \n3619: Form + Untimed\n3620: → comprehensive chapter assessment\n3621: \n3622: Form + Timed\n3623: → concise, balanced complete assessment\n3624: \n3625: Identification Tab\n3626: → terminology and clearly identifiable concepts\n3627: ```\n3628: \n3629: These are generation strategies, not merely different UI skins.\n3630: \n3631: ## 147. Final Generator Principle\n3632: \n3633: The exam generator should behave like an assessment designer, not a random question picker.\n3634: \n3635: The priority order is:\n3636: \n3637: 1. source accuracy;\n3638: 2. learning-objective and concept coverage;\n3639: 3. unambiguous answerability;\n3640: 4. appropriate question type;\n3641: 5. mode/time suitability;\n3642: 6. difficulty and cognitive balance;\n3643: 7. low redundancy;\n3644: 8. meaningful variation;\n3645: 9. randomness only among equally suitable candidates.\n3646: \n3647: A generated exam is considered successful only when it is both different enough from previous attempts and educationally equivalent in the concepts it assesses.\n3648: \n3649: ## 148. Additional Final Delivery Checklist — Intelligent Generation\n3650: \n3651: Before delivery, verify:\n3652: \n3653: - [ ] Questions were selected through a blueprint rather than simple random sampling.\n3654: - [ ] Important concepts are represented.\n3655: - [ ] Major topics are represented proportionally to the source.\n3656: - [ ] Duplicate and near-duplicate concepts are controlled.\n3657: - [ ] Interactive mode has questions suitable for immediate validation.\n3658: - [ ] Form mode forms a coherent complete assessment.\n3659: - [ ] Timed mode respects the configured time constraint.\n3660: - [ ] Untimed mode can use deeper assessment when supported.\n3661: - [ ] All four Experience × Time combinations are handled appropriately.\n3662: - [ ] Question types are selected according to the concept being tested.\n3663: - [ ] Identification questions have defensible accepted answers.\n3664: - [ ] Enumeration questions are source-grounded and properly scored.\n3665: - [ ] True/False statements are unambiguous.\n3666: - [ ] Multiple Choice distractors are plausible and non-overlapping.\n3667: - [ ] Difficulty is intentional rather than random.\n3668: - [ ] Cognitive demand is balanced where the source allows it.\n3669: - [ ] Previous performance can inform future Interactive exams when available.\n3670: - [ ] Randomness is used only as a final variation mechanism.\n3671: - [ ] Failed audits trigger targeted replacement rather than blind reshuffling.\n3672: - [ ] Overall Exam uses its own cross-chapter blueprint.\n3673: - [ ] Identification-only tab uses intelligent Identification selection.\n\n(End of file - total 3673 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## 99. Professional Visual Design\n\nThe examination system MUST have a professional, polished academic/exam-platform appearance.\n\nThe design should communicate:\n\n- Serious examination purpose\n- Clear information hierarchy\n- Easy reading\n- Consistent spacing\n- Consistent typography\n- Strong visual grouping\n- Clear interactive states\n- Responsive behavior\n\nUse a restrained, professional visual system rather than decorative effects.\n\nAvoid:\n\n- Excessive gradients","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\.opencode\\skills\\interactive-comprehensive-exam-generator\\SKILL.md","text":"## 99. Professional Visual Design\n\nThe examination system MUST have a professional, polished academic/exam-platform appearance.\n\nThe design should communicate:\n\n- Serious examination purpose\n- Clear information hierarchy\n- Easy reading\n- Consistent spacing\n- Consistent typography\n- Strong visual grouping\n- Clear interactive states\n- Responsive behavior\n\nUse a restrained, professional visual system rather than decorative effects.\n\nAvoid:\n\n- Excessive gradients\n- Excessive animations\n- Unnecessary glassmorphism\n- Distracting illustrations\n- Oversized decorative elements\n- Cluttered dashboards\n- Inconsistent colors\n- Excessive rounded cards\n- Visual effects that reduce readability\n\nThe interface should feel like a modern professional assessment platform rather than a casual game.\n\nUse visual hierarchy for:\n\n```text\nApplication Header\n    ↓\nNavigation / Chapter Tabs\n    ↓\nExam Title + Description\n    ↓\nMode Selection\n    ↓\nQuestion Area\n    ↓\nAnswer Controls\n    ↓\nFeedback / Submission\n    ↓\nProgress / Results\n```\n\nMaintain consistent styling across:\n\n- Buttons\n- Tabs\n- Cards\n- Inputs\n- Radio buttons\n- Checkboxes\n- Text fields\n- Timers\n- Progress indicators\n- Feedback messages\n- Results screens\n\nAll states should have visually distinct but accessible:\n\n- Default\n- Hover\n- Focus\n- Selected\n- Correct\n- Incorrect\n- Disabled\n- Submitted\n- Locked\n\nDo not communicate correctness through color alone. Pair color with text, icons, borders, or other accessible indicators.\n\n---\n\n## 100. Exam Experience Modes\n\nThe system must support TWO independent exam experience modes.\n\nThese modes describe HOW the exam is taken, not whether it is timed.\n\n### Mode A — Interactive / One-by-One\n\nThis mode should behave like a flashcard or Quizizz-style examination.\n\nDisplay only one primary question at a time.\n\nFlow:\n\n```text\nQuestion\n   ↓\nStudent answers\n   ↓\nSubmit / Select Answer\n   ↓\nImmediate validation\n   ↓\nCorrect / Incorrect feedback\n   ↓\nCorrect answer + concise explanation\n   ↓\nQuestion becomes locked\n   ↓\nNext Question\n```\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Show one question at a time.\n- Validate the answer immediately.\n- Immediately display whether the answer is correct or incorrect.\n- Reveal the correct answer after submission.\n- Show a concise explanation when available.\n- Update score immediately.\n- Lock the submitted question.\n- Prevent changing an already submitted answer.\n- Provide a clear `Next` control.\n- Show current question number and total.\n- Show progress.\n- Show score where appropriate.\n\nThe experience should feel sequential:\n\n```text\nQ1 → Answer → Feedback → Next\nQ2 → Answer → Feedback → Next\nQ3 → Answer → Feedback → Next\n```\n\nDo not show the entire chapter as a long form in this mode.\n\n### Mode B — Form / One-Time Submit\n\nThis mode should behave like Google Forms.\n\nDisplay multiple questions vertically in a scrollable form.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Show the questions together.\n- Allow the student to answer questions without immediate correctness feedback.\n- Do not reveal answers while the form is being completed.\n- Provide one final `Submit Exam` action.\n- Validate the entire exam only after submission.\n- Calculate the final score after submission.\n- Show results after submission.\n- Optionally show per-question corrections after submission.\n- Lock the submitted attempt unless the user explicitly resets/restarts it.\n\nThe student should experience:\n\n```text\nQuestion 1\nQuestion 2\nQuestion 3\n...\nQuestion N\n        ↓\nSubmit Exam\n        ↓\nValidate All\n        ↓\nResults\n```\n\nDo not accidentally make this mode behave like Interactive mode.\n\n---\n\n## 101. Experience Mode Independence\n\nExperience mode and time mode are separate settings.\n\nThe system must support combinations such as:\n\n```text\nInteractive + Timed\nInteractive + Untimed\n\nForm + Timed\nForm + Untimed\n```\n\nIf the implementation does not support a particular combination for a specific exam type, the UI must clearly disable that combination rather than pretending it works.\n\nDo not confuse:\n\n```text\nExperience Mode:\nInteractive / Form\n\nTime Mode:\nTimed / Untimed\n```\n\nThey must have independent state.\n\nExample:\n\n```javascript\nexamSettings = {\n    experienceMode: \"interactive\",\n    timeMode: \"untimed\"\n};\n```\n\nA Timed exam can still be Interactive or Form-like.\n\nAn Untimed exam can still be Interactive or Form-like.\n\n---\n\n## 102. Existing Timed and Untimed Modes\n\nTimed and Untimed modes ALREADY EXIST in this specification.\n\nPreserve all requirements from Sections 5–8.\n\nTimed:\n\n```text\n10 seconds per question\n```\n\nUntimed:\n\n```text\nNo countdown\nNo timeout\nUnlimited answering time\n```\n\nDo not replace the existing Timed/Untimed system with the new Interactive/Form modes.\n\nInstead, combine them as separate dimensions:\n\n```text\nChapter\n ├── Experience Mode\n │    ├── Interactive\n │    └── Form\n │\n └── Time Mode\n      ├── Timed\n      └── Untimed\n```\n\n---\n\n## 103. Chapter Exam-Type Selection\n\nEach chapter must support different examination types.\n\nSupported types:\n\n```text\nMultiple Choice\nIdentification\nTrue or False\nEnumeration\n```\n\nA chapter may use one type or a deliberate mixture of types.\n\nDo not force every chapter to use the same exam type.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nChapter 1 → Multiple Choice\nChapter 2 → Identification\nChapter 3 → True or False\nChapter 4 → Enumeration\n```\n\nAnother valid configuration:\n\n```text\nChapter 1\n    Multiple Choice\n    Identification\n    True or False\n\nChapter 2\n    Multiple Choice\n    Enumeration\n```\n\nThe selected exam type must be clearly shown before the exam begins.\n\n---\n\n## 104. Multiple Choice\n\nMultiple Choice questions must follow the existing requirements in Sections 13–45.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Exactly four choices\n- A, B, C, D\n- Exactly one correct answer\n- Plausible distractors\n- Balanced answer positions\n- No answer-pattern clues\n- Immediate validation in Interactive mode\n- Batch validation in Form mode\n\nDo not apply four-choice requirements to Identification, True/False, or Enumeration questions.\n\n---\n\n## 105. Identification Questions\n\nIdentification questions require the student to provide the answer rather than select from choices.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nQuestion:\nWhat command displays the current working directory?\n\nAnswer:\npwd\n```\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Use a text input.\n- Do not show answer choices.\n- Validate according to the reference.\n- Ignore irrelevant capitalization when appropriate.\n- Normalize harmless surrounding whitespace.\n- Do not accept obviously different concepts merely because they are vaguely related.\n- Support documented alternate answers when the reference clearly permits them.\n\nExample normalization:\n\n```text\n\" PWD \"\n\"pwd\"\n\"Pwd\"\n```\n\nmay be treated equivalently when capitalization is not conceptually meaningful.\n\nFor answers where capitalization matters according to the reference, preserve the distinction.\n\nIdentification validation should support an explicit answer structure:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    type: \"identification\",\n    acceptedAnswers: [\"pwd\"],\n    explanation: \"...\"\n}\n```\n\nIf multiple equivalent answers are valid:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    type: \"identification\",\n    acceptedAnswers: [\n        \"answer one\",\n        \"answer 1\"\n    ]\n}\n```\n\nDo not invent alternate answers.\n\n---\n\n## 106. Identification Feedback\n\nInteractive Identification:\n\n```text\nStudent enters answer\n        ↓\nSubmit\n        ↓\nValidate\n        ↓\nCorrect / Incorrect\n        ↓\nShow accepted answer\n        ↓\nExplanation\n        ↓\nNext\n```\n\nForm Identification:\n\n```text\nStudent enters all answers\n        ↓\nSubmit Exam\n        ↓\nValidate all answers\n        ↓\nShow results\n```\n\nDo not reveal the correct answer before submission.\n\n---\n\n## 107. True or False Questions\n\nTrue or False questions must contain exactly two possible answers:\n\n```text\nTrue\nFalse\n```\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Statement must be supported by the reference.\n- Only one answer may be correct.\n- Avoid trivial wording.\n- Avoid unsupported absolute statements.\n- Avoid ambiguity.\n- Clearly identify the statement being judged.\n- Test actual understanding rather than guessing from wording.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n[ True ] [ False ]\n\nThe reference states that ______.\n```\n\nThe correct answer must be explicitly supported by the source material.\n\n---\n\n## 108. Enumeration Questions\n\nEnumeration questions require the student to provide multiple items.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nQuestion:\nList the three categories identified in the reference.\n\nAnswer:\n1. ______\n2. ______\n3. ______\n```\n\nRequirements:\n\n- The required number of items must be explicit when the reference provides a fixed number.\n- Define the expected answer items in structured data.\n- Validate each required item.\n- Ignore harmless capitalization and surrounding whitespace when appropriate.\n- Do not require a specific order unless the reference establishes that order as meaningful.\n- If order is meaningful, preserve and validate order.\n- Do not invent additional acceptable items.\n\nExample:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    type: \"enumeration\",\n    requiredItems: [\n        \"item one\",\n        \"item two\",\n        \"item three\"\n    ],\n    orderMatters: false\n}\n```\n\nFor order-sensitive material:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    type: \"enumeration\",\n    requiredItems: [\n        \"step one\",\n        \"step two\",\n        \"step three\"\n    ],\n    orderMatters: true\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 109. Enumeration Scoring\n\nEnumeration scoring must be explicit and consistent.\n\nFor example, if five items are required:\n\n```text\n5/5 → Full credit\n4/5 → Partial credit\n3/5 → Partial credit\n...\n```\n\nIf the exam uses all-or-nothing scoring, state that clearly in the interface or exam configuration.\n\nDo not silently switch scoring behavior between chapters.\n\nExample:\n\n```javascript\nscoring: {\n    type: \"partial\",\n    pointsPerItem: 1\n}\n```\n\nor:\n\n```javascript\nscoring: {\n    type: \"allOrNothing\"\n}\n```\n\nThe chosen scoring model must be consistent for that exam.\n\n---\n\n## 110. Exam-Type Metadata\n\nEvery question should identify its question type.\n\nExample:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    id: \"ch1-q01\",\n    chapter: 1,\n    type: \"multipleChoice\"\n}\n```\n\nSupported values:\n\n```text\nmultipleChoice\nidentification\ntrueFalse\nenumeration\n```\n\nAdditional metadata may include:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    id: \"ch1-q02\",\n    chapter: 1,\n    type: \"identification\",\n    topic: \"Topic Name\",\n    difficulty: \"hard\",\n    bloomLevel: \"apply\"\n}\n```\n\nThe UI must render the correct answer control based on `type`.\n\n---\n\n## 111. Question-Type Diversity\n\nWhen a chapter uses multiple exam types, distribute them deliberately.\n\nAvoid generating an arbitrary mixture.\n\nA reasonable example:\n\n```text\n30-question chapter\n\nMultiple Choice   → 15\nIdentification    → 7\nTrue or False     → 5\nEnumeration       → 3\n```\n\nThe exact distribution may change depending on the reference.\n\nPrioritize:\n\n1. Reference coverage\n2. Assessment quality\n3. Suitability of question type\n4. Difficulty\n5. Variety\n\nDo not force a question into a type that does not fit the material.\n\n---\n\n## 112. Dedicated Identification Tab\n\nIn addition to the normal chapter tabs, provide a dedicated:\n\n```text\nIdentification\n```\n\ntab.\n\nThis tab is specifically for identification examinations across chapters.\n\nNavigation should resemble:\n\n```text\nChapter 1\nChapter 2\nChapter 3\nChapter 4\nIdentification\nOverall Exam\nAnswer Key\nEncyclopedia\n```\n\nThe Identification tab must NOT simply duplicate one chapter's normal exam.\n\nIt should provide access to identification questions from every chapter.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nIdentification\n\nChapter 1\n[Start Identification Exam]\n\nChapter 2\n[Start Identification Exam]\n\nChapter 3\n[Start Identification Exam]\n\nChapter 4\n[Start Identification Exam]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 113. Identification Tab Behavior\n\nThe dedicated Identification tab must:\n\n- Show available chapters.\n- Show the number of identification questions for each chapter.\n- Allow the user to start an identification exam for a selected chapter.\n- Use text-input answers.\n- Support Interactive and Form experience modes when configured.\n- Support Timed and Untimed modes when configured.\n- Validate answers according to Identification rules.\n- Display results after completion.\n- Preserve the original chapter identification question bank.\n- Not alter the normal chapter exam state.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nIdentification\n────────────────────────────\n\nChapter 1\n8 questions\n[Start]\n\nChapter 2\n6 questions\n[Start]\n\nChapter 3\n10 questions\n[Start]\n```\n\nThe dedicated Identification tab is a separate examination entry point.\n\n---\n\n## 114. Identification State Independence\n\nIdentification exams must maintain independent state.\n\nDo not reuse:\n\n```text\nchapter multiple-choice score\nchapter currentQuestion\nchapter selectedAnswer\nchapter timer\nchapter completion state\n```\n\nunless the implementation explicitly creates a separate Identification attempt state.\n\nExample:\n\n```javascript\nidentificationExamState = {\n    chapter: 1,\n    experienceMode: \"interactive\",\n    timeMode: \"untimed\",\n    currentQuestion: 0,\n    score: 0,\n    completed: false\n};\n```\n\nA completed Multiple Choice exam must not automatically mark its Identification exam as completed.\n\n---\n\n## 115. Navigation and Active Exam Locking\n\nThe existing chapter-locking rules remain active for every exam type and experience mode.\n\nWhile an exam is active, prevent:\n\n- Switching to another chapter\n- Opening another exam\n- Opening the Overall Exam\n- Opening the Answer Key\n- Opening answer-revealing reference material\n- Changing experience mode\n- Changing time mode\n\nThis applies to:\n\n```text\nMultiple Choice\nIdentification\nTrue or False\nEnumeration\n```\n\nand:\n\n```text\nInteractive\nForm\n```\n\nAfter completion or reset, normal navigation becomes available.\n\n---\n\n## 116. Mode Selection Screen\n\nBefore starting a chapter exam, display a professional configuration screen.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nChapter 1\nDatabase Fundamentals\n\nExam Type\n[ Multiple Choice ]\n\nExperience\n(●) Interactive\n(○) Form\n\nTime\n(●) Untimed\n(○) Timed — 10 seconds/question\n\nQuestions\n30\n\n[ Start Exam ]\n```\n\nFor an exam that supports multiple question types:\n\n```text\nExam Type\n(●) Mixed\n(○) Multiple Choice\n(○) Identification\n(○) True or False\n(○) Enumeration\n```\n\nOnly display configuration options that are actually supported by that exam.\n\n---\n\n## 117. Mixed Exam Mode\n\nIf Mixed exam type is enabled, questions may contain:\n\n```text\nMultiple Choice\nIdentification\nTrue or False\nEnumeration\n```\n\nThe question interface must automatically change according to the active question type.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nQ1 → Multiple Choice\n    [A] [B] [C] [D]\n\nQ2 → Identification\n    [Text Input]\n\nQ3 → True or False\n    [True] [False]\n\nQ4 → Enumeration\n    [Item 1]\n    [Item 2]\n    [Item 3]\n```\n\nDo not display irrelevant controls.\n\n---\n\n## 118. Form Mode Submission Rules by Question Type\n\nIn Form mode, all supported question types must be submitted together.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nQuestion 1 — Multiple Choice\n○ A\n○ B\n○ C\n○ D\n\nQuestion 2 — Identification\n[____________]\n\nQuestion 3 — True or False\n○ True\n○ False\n\nQuestion 4 — Enumeration\n1. [__________]\n2. [__________]\n3. [__________]\n\n[Submit Exam]\n```\n\nNo question should receive correctness feedback before the final submission.\n\nAfter submission:\n\n- Lock all inputs.\n- Calculate results.\n- Show score.\n- Show question-level correctness.\n- Show correct answers where appropriate.\n- Show explanations.\n- Provide Review/Reset controls.\n\n---\n\n## 119. Interactive Mode Submission Rules by Question Type\n\nInteractive mode must validate each question independently.\n\nMultiple Choice:\n\n```text\nSelect choice\n→ Immediate validation\n```\n\nIdentification:\n\n```text\nEnter answer\n→ Submit\n→ Immediate validation\n```\n\nTrue or False:\n\n```text\nSelect True/False\n→ Immediate validation\n```\n\nEnumeration:\n\n```text\nEnter required items\n→ Submit\n→ Immediate validation\n```\n\nAfter validation, lock the current question and allow the user to continue.\n\n---\n\n## 120. Results by Exam Type\n\nResults should clearly report performance appropriate to the question types used.\n\nAt minimum:\n\n```text\nFinal Score\nCorrect\nIncorrect\nUnanswered\nPercentage\n```\n\nFor mixed or partial-credit exams, also show:\n\n```text\nPoints Earned\nPoints Possible\n```\n\nFor Identification and Enumeration, provide useful review information without exposing answers before submission.\n\n---\n\n## 121. Professional Empty and Error States\n\nThe interface must handle unusual states professionally.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nNo identification questions available for this chapter.\n```\n\n```text\nThis exam type is not available for this chapter.\n```\n\n```text\nPlease answer all required items before submitting.\n```\n\n```text\nNo questions were generated because the reference does not support this exam type.\n```\n\nDo not show blank screens, undefined values, broken controls, or fake question counts.\n\n---\n\n## 122. Updated Navigation Requirement\n\nThe complete navigation should support:\n\n```text\nChapter Tabs\n├── Chapter 1\n├── Chapter 2\n├── Chapter 3\n└── ...\n\nDedicated Exam Tabs\n├── Identification\n└── Overall Exam\n\nUtility Tabs\n├── Answer Key\n└── Encyclopedia\n```\n\nIf additional exam-type tabs are implemented later, they must have a clear purpose and must not duplicate existing functionality unnecessarily.\n\n---\n\n## 123. Updated Final Delivery Checklist\n\nAdd the following requirements to the existing checklist:\n\n```text\n[ ] Professional academic/exam-platform visual design\n[ ] Responsive professional layout\n[ ] Interactive / One-by-One experience mode works\n[ ] Form / Google Forms-like experience mode works\n[ ] Interactive mode validates immediately\n[ ] Interactive mode locks answered questions\n[ ] Form mode validates only after final submission\n[ ] Form mode locks after submission\n[ ] Experience mode and Time mode are independent\n[ ] Timed + Interactive works\n[ ] Untimed + Interactive works\n[ ] Timed + Form works when supported\n[ ] Untimed + Form works\n[ ] Multiple Choice supported\n[ ] Identification supported\n[ ] True or False supported\n[ ] Enumeration supported\n[ ] Question type metadata is correct\n[ ] Identification validation is normalized and reference-grounded\n[ ] Enumeration scoring is explicitly defined\n[ ] Mixed exam type works when enabled\n[ ] Dedicated Identification tab exists\n[ ] Identification tab contains exams for each chapter\n[ ] Identification state is independent\n[ ] Navigation locking applies to all exam types\n[ ] Mode-selection screen clearly separates Experience Mode from Time Mode\n[ ] Results work for all supported question types\n[ ] Empty/error states are handled professionally\n```\n\n---\n\n## 124. Final Experience Requirement\n\nThe completed system should provide two distinct ways to study and assess each chapter:\n\n### Interactive Study/Exam\n\n```text\nChoose Chapter\n      ↓\nChoose Exam Type\n      ↓\nChoose Interactive\n      ↓\nChoose Timed / Untimed\n      ↓\nStart\n      ↓\nOne question\n      ↓\nAnswer\n      ↓\nImmediate validation\n      ↓\nFeedback\n      ↓\nNext question\n```\n\n### Form-Style Exam\n\n```text\nChoose Chapter\n      ↓\nChoose Exam Type\n      ↓\nChoose Form\n      ↓\nChoose Timed / Untimed\n      ↓\nStart\n      ↓\nComplete full form\n      ↓\nSubmit once\n      ↓\nValidate entire exam\n      ↓\nResults\n```\n\nThe system must make these experiences visibly and behaviorally different.\n\nThe final product should feel professional enough for serious academic review while remaining simple enough for a student to understand immediately.\n\nThe goal is not merely to generate questions. The goal is to provide a complete, reference-grounded assessment platform with multiple examination types, multiple interaction modes, independent timed/untimed behavior, chapter-specific assessments, and a dedicated identification examination area.\n\n\n## 125. Intelligent Exam Generation — Core Requirement\n\nThe exam generator MUST NOT select questions using simple random sampling alone. Randomness may be used only after the generator has determined that the selected set satisfies the required learning coverage and quality constraints.\n\nThe generator's primary goal is useful assessment, not merely variety.\n\nFor every generated exam, the generator MUST first analyze the available reference material and build a question pool containing:\n\n- source section/chapter\n- topic/concept\n- learning target or fact being tested\n- question type\n- difficulty\n- cognitive demand\n- importance/centrality of the concept\n- whether the concept has already been tested\n- acceptable answer(s), when applicable\n- source/reference location\n- potential distractor quality, for Multiple Choice\n\nIf the source material does not contain enough information to support a requested question, the generator MUST NOT invent one merely to reach a target question count.\n\n## 126. Exam Blueprint Before Question Selection\n\nBefore generating questions, create an internal exam blueprint.\n\nThe blueprint MUST determine:\n\n1. which major topics must be represented;\n2. how many questions each topic should receive;\n3. which important concepts require direct assessment;\n4. which concepts can be assessed through application or comparison;\n5. which question types are appropriate for each concept;\n6. the intended difficulty distribution;\n7. the intended cognitive-demand distribution;\n8. which concepts should not be repeated unnecessarily;\n9. which questions should be reserved for other modes/exams;\n10. the final number of questions.\n\nThe generator should prefer broad and meaningful coverage over repeatedly asking about the same easy facts.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nChapter coverage\n├── Topic A — 20%\n├── Topic B — 25%\n├── Topic C — 30%\n└── Topic D — 25%\n\nDifficulty\n├── Easy — 30%\n├── Medium — 50%\n└── Hard — 20%\n\nCognitive demand\n├── Recall — 30%\n├── Understanding — 40%\n└── Application/Analysis — 30%\n```\n\nThese percentages are defaults, not mandatory values. The generator MUST adapt them to the actual source material.\n\n## 127. Concept Coverage and Anti-Redundancy\n\nThe generator MUST track concepts rather than treating every question as independent.\n\nEach question should contain metadata similar to:\n\n```js\n{\n  topic: \"permissions\",\n  concept: \"chmod numeric permissions\",\n  sourceSection: \"File Permissions\",\n  type: \"multipleChoice\",\n  difficulty: \"medium\",\n  cognitiveLevel: \"application\",\n  importance: \"high\"\n}\n```\n\nThe generator MUST avoid producing several questions that test the same fact using only superficial wording changes.\n\nFor example, these should count as substantially the same concept:\n\n```text\nWhat does chmod 755 do?\nWhat permissions does 755 provide?\nWhich permission mode is equivalent to rwxr-xr-x?\n```\n\nOnly one or a limited number should normally appear in the same exam unless repetition is intentionally required for mastery.\n\n## 128. Intelligent Generation for Interactive Mode\n\nInteractive / One-by-One mode MUST use questions that work well with immediate feedback.\n\nThe generator should prioritize:\n\n- clear, focused questions;\n- one concept at a time;\n- questions that can be confidently validated immediately;\n- meaningful distractors for Multiple Choice;\n- concise expected answers for Identification;\n- unambiguous True/False statements;\n- manageable Enumeration prompts.\n\nInteractive mode SHOULD progressively vary the cognitive demand rather than presenting a sequence of trivial recall questions.\n\nA typical generated sequence should intentionally mix:\n\n1. foundational recall;\n2. understanding;\n3. application;\n4. comparison or interpretation;\n5. targeted reinforcement of weak areas when the system supports adaptive behavior.\n\nThe generator MUST NOT simply shuffle the same fixed question bank and call that adaptive.\n\n## 129. Intelligent Generation for Form / One-Time Submit Mode\n\nForm / One-Time Submit mode MUST be designed as a coherent complete assessment.\n\nThe generator should prioritize:\n\n- comprehensive topic coverage;\n- balanced difficulty;\n- low redundancy;\n- logical ordering;\n- a mixture of question types when configured;\n- enough questions to measure the chapter without unnecessary repetition.\n\nThe form should normally progress from foundational questions toward more demanding questions, unless the configured exam blueprint specifies another order.\n\nThe generator MUST avoid placing several nearly identical questions next to one another.\n\n## 130. Intelligent Generation for Timed Mode\n\nTimed mode MUST account for the limited time available.\n\nQuestion selection MUST consider:\n\n- expected reading time;\n- expected answer time;\n- question complexity;\n- enumeration length;\n- identification ambiguity;\n- number of choices;\n- cognitive demand.\n\nTimed exams SHOULD avoid requiring unusually long responses when the time limit is short.\n\nFor the existing default of 10 seconds per question, the generator should strongly favor questions that can reasonably be read, understood, and answered within that constraint.\n\nLong Enumeration and complex Identification prompts SHOULD be excluded from a 10-second Timed exam unless the reference material and configuration clearly justify them.\n\nThe generator MUST NOT create a Timed exam by taking an Untimed exam and simply adding a timer.\n\n## 131. Intelligent Generation for Untimed Mode\n\nUntimed mode can support deeper assessment.\n\nThe generator may include:\n\n- more complex application questions;\n- comparison questions;\n- longer Identification prompts when supported by the material;\n- Enumeration requiring several source-supported items;\n- questions requiring careful interpretation.\n\nHowever, Untimed MUST still prioritize useful coverage and must not become a collection of unnecessarily difficult questions.\n\n## 132. Intelligent Generation by Experience × Time Combination\n\nExperience Mode and Time Mode are independent, but question generation MUST account for their combination.\n\nThe generator MUST evaluate these four combinations separately:\n\n```text\nInteractive + Timed\nInteractive + Untimed\nForm + Timed\nForm + Untimed\n```\n\nEach combination may use a different generated question set from the same source material.\n\nThe system MUST NOT assume that one universal randomized question list is appropriate for all four combinations.\n\n## 133. Intelligent Generation by Question Type\n\nQuestion type selection MUST be concept-driven.\n\n### Multiple Choice\n\nUse Multiple Choice when:\n\n- the source supports clear alternatives;\n- distractors can be constructed from plausible misunderstandings or related concepts;\n- exactly one answer can be defended from the source.\n\nDistractors MUST be plausible but clearly incorrect according to the reference material.\n\nDo not create distractors that are obviously absurd merely to fill four choices.\n\n### Identification\n\nUse Identification when the target is a:\n\n- term;\n- command;\n- concept;\n- definition;\n- named item;\n- clearly identifiable fact.\n\nIdentification should NOT be used when multiple answers could reasonably satisfy the prompt unless all accepted answers are explicitly defined.\n\n### True or False\n\nUse True/False for precise statements where the source clearly supports either True or False.\n\nAvoid statements that depend on:\n\n- vague wording;\n- hidden exceptions;\n- unsupported assumptions;\n- subjective interpretation.\n\n### Enumeration\n\nUse Enumeration when the reference explicitly provides:\n\n- a list;\n- several required steps;\n- multiple components;\n- categories;\n- options/items that should be recalled together.\n\nThe generator MUST NOT invent additional enumeration items.\n\n## 134. Cross-Type Coverage\n\nWhen a chapter supports multiple question types, the generator should test the same important concept from different useful angles only when doing so adds assessment value.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nConcept: chmod numeric permissions\n\nIdentification:\n\"What command changes file permissions?\"\n\nMultiple Choice:\n\"Which mode represents rwxr-xr-x?\"\n\nTrue/False:\n\"chmod 755 gives the owner read, write, and execute permissions.\"\n\nEnumeration:\n\"List the permission values represented by r, w, and x.\"\n```\n\nThese should NOT all automatically appear in the same exam.\n\nThe generator should select the representation that best measures the intended learning target and use another representation only when it tests a distinct skill.\n\n## 135. Difficulty Generation\n\nDifficulty MUST be determined from the source-supported reasoning required, not from arbitrary wording.\n\nSuggested criteria:\n\n### Easy\n- direct recall;\n- explicit definition;\n- directly stated fact;\n- straightforward recognition.\n\n### Medium\n- interpretation;\n- comparison;\n- applying a documented rule;\n- selecting between plausible alternatives.\n\n### Hard\n- multi-step application;\n- combining multiple source-supported concepts;\n- interpreting a non-obvious but defensible scenario;\n- distinguishing closely related concepts.\n\nA question MUST NOT be labeled Hard merely because it is obscure or intentionally confusing.\n\n## 136. Cognitive-Demand Balance\n\nThe generator should track cognitive demand separately from difficulty.\n\nPossible levels:\n\n```text\nRecall\nUnderstanding\nApplication\nAnalysis\n```\n\nThe generator should avoid making an entire exam Recall-only when the source material supports deeper questions.\n\nHowever, it MUST NOT force Application or Analysis questions when the reference material does not provide enough information to support them.\n\n## 137. Learning-Value Scoring for Candidate Questions\n\nEvery candidate question should receive an internal quality/utility assessment before selection.\n\nSuggested factors:\n\n```text\ncoverageValue\nimportanceValue\ncognitiveValue\ndifficultyFit\nquestionTypeFit\nsourceConfidence\nredundancyPenalty\nambiguityPenalty\ntimeCost\n```\n\nA conceptual selection score may be calculated as:\n\n```text\nutility =\n  coverageValue\n  + importanceValue\n  + cognitiveValue\n  + difficultyFit\n  + questionTypeFit\n  + sourceConfidence\n  - redundancyPenalty\n  - ambiguityPenalty\n  - timeCostPenalty\n```\n\nThe exact numeric weights are implementation details. The important requirement is that question selection MUST consider these factors rather than selecting candidates uniformly at random.\n\n## 138. Randomness as a Final Tie-Breaker\n\nRandom selection MAY be used only after candidate questions have passed quality and blueprint constraints.\n\nCorrect sequence:\n\n```text\nSource Material\n      ↓\nExtract Concepts\n      ↓\nBuild Question Candidates\n      ↓\nValidate Candidates\n      ↓\nBuild Exam Blueprint\n      ↓\nFilter Low-Value / Redundant Questions\n      ↓\nMatch Coverage + Difficulty + Cognitive Demand\n      ↓\nSelect High-Utility Questions\n      ↓\nUse Randomness Only Among Comparable Candidates\n      ↓\nRun Final Validation\n      ↓\nGenerate Exam\n```\n\nIncorrect sequence:\n\n```text\nQuestion Bank\n      ↓\nRandom.shuffle()\n      ↓\nTake first N questions\n```\n\nThe second approach MUST NOT be used as the primary exam-generation strategy.\n\n## 139. Adaptive Reinforcement Without Random Noise\n\nIf the system tracks user performance, future Interactive exams MAY use previous results to reinforce weak concepts.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPrevious result:\nPermissions = weak\nArchives = strong\nNavigation = strong\n```\n\nThe next Interactive exam may increase coverage of Permissions while still retaining broader chapter coverage.\n\nThe system MUST NOT turn this into repetitive drilling of one question.\n\nAdaptive selection should target:\n\n- weak concepts;\n- missed question types;\n- misunderstood distinctions;\n- underrepresented high-importance topics.\n\nIt should still maintain balanced coverage.\n\n## 140. Per-Mode Question Bank Separation\n\nThe generator should maintain logical pools:\n\n```text\nchapterBank\n├── interactiveBank\n├── formBank\n├── timedBank\n├── untimedBank\n├── identificationBank\n├── multipleChoiceBank\n├── trueFalseBank\n└── enumerationBank\n```\n\nThese are logical filtered views and do not necessarily require duplicated physical questions.\n\nA question may belong to multiple compatible pools, but each exam generation pass MUST apply the rules for the selected mode.\n\n## 141. Exam Generation Audit\n\nBefore displaying an exam, automatically audit the generated set.\n\nThe audit MUST check:\n\n- requested question count;\n- chapter/topic coverage;\n- major concept coverage;\n- question-type distribution;\n- difficulty distribution;\n- cognitive-demand distribution;\n- duplicate concepts;\n- near-duplicate wording;\n- answer uniqueness;\n- source support;\n- distractor quality;\n- Timed feasibility;\n- Identification accepted answers;\n- Enumeration scoring;\n- True/False ambiguity;\n- no unsupported facts.\n\nIf the audit fails, regenerate or replace only the problematic questions.\n\nDo NOT blindly regenerate the entire exam with a new random seed, because that can reintroduce the same quality problems.\n\n## 142. Regeneration Strategy\n\nWhen an exam fails validation:\n\n1. identify the exact failed constraint;\n2. identify the question(s) responsible;\n3. remove only invalid or low-utility candidates;\n4. select replacement candidates that improve the failed constraint;\n5. rerun the audit;\n6. repeat until valid or until the source material is exhausted.\n\nIf the source cannot satisfy a requested blueprint, the system should report the limitation rather than inventing content.\n\n## 143. Exam-to-Exam Variation\n\nDifferent attempts MAY vary question selection, but variation must preserve educational coverage.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nAttempt 1:\nTopic A → Q1\nTopic B → Q4\nTopic C → Q7\n\nAttempt 2:\nTopic A → Q3\nTopic B → Q8\nTopic C → Q10\n```\n\nBoth attempts should still assess the important concepts.\n\nThe generator MUST NOT interpret \"different exam\" as \"completely different random questions with no coverage guarantees.\"\n\n## 144. Chapter-Level Blueprint Inheritance\n\nEach chapter should first receive its own blueprint based on the actual content of that chapter.\n\nThe Overall Exam should then construct a higher-level blueprint from all chapters.\n\nThe Overall Exam MUST NOT simply concatenate random questions from each chapter.\n\nIt should consider:\n\n- chapter representation;\n- overall topic coverage;\n- duplicate concepts across chapters;\n- total assessment length;\n- question-type balance;\n- difficulty progression;\n- form-style vertical presentation requirements.\n\n## 145. Identification-Only Tab Generation\n\nThe dedicated Identification tab MUST use the same intelligent generation principles.\n\nIt should NOT simply extract random questions and convert them into Identification format.\n\nFor each chapter:\n\n1. identify concepts suitable for Identification;\n2. verify that the concept has a clear expected answer;\n3. define accepted answer variants where justified;\n4. remove ambiguous concepts;\n5. balance coverage across important terms;\n6. generate the Identification exam;\n7. validate answers against the source.\n\n## 146. Mode-Specific Generation Examples\n\nThe implementation should conceptually support results such as:\n\n```text\nInteractive + Untimed\n→ deeper application and immediate reinforcement\n\nInteractive + Timed\n→ concise, quickly answerable questions\n\nForm + Untimed\n→ comprehensive chapter assessment\n\nForm + Timed\n→ concise, balanced complete assessment\n\nIdentification Tab\n→ terminology and clearly identifiable concepts\n```\n\nThese are generation strategies, not merely different UI skins.\n\n## 147. Final Generator Principle\n\nThe exam generator should behave like an assessment designer, not a random question picker.\n\nThe priority order is:\n\n1. source accuracy;\n2. learning-objective and concept coverage;\n3. unambiguous answerability;\n4. appropriate question type;\n5. mode/time suitability;\n6. difficulty and cognitive balance;\n7. low redundancy;\n8. meaningful variation;\n9. randomness only among equally suitable candidates.\n\nA generated exam is considered successful only when it is both different enough from previous attempts and educationally equivalent in the concepts it assesses.\n\n## 148. Additional Final Delivery Checklist — Intelligent Generation\n\nBefore delivery, verify:\n\n- [ ] Questions were selected through a blueprint rather than simple random sampling.\n- [ ] Important concepts are represented.\n- [ ] Major topics are represented proportionally to the source.\n- [ ] Duplicate and near-duplicate concepts are controlled.\n- [ ] Interactive mode has questions suitable for immediate validation.\n- [ ] Form mode forms a coherent complete assessment.\n- [ ] Timed mode respects the configured time constraint.\n- [ ] Untimed mode can use deeper assessment when supported.\n- [ ] All four Experience × Time combinations are handled appropriately.\n- [ ] Question types are selected according to the concept being tested.\n- [ ] Identification questions have defensible accepted answers.\n- [ ] Enumeration questions are source-grounded and properly scored.\n- [ ] True/False statements are unambiguous.\n- [ ] Multiple Choice distractors are plausible and non-overlapping.\n- [ ] Difficulty is intentional rather than random.\n- [ ] Cognitive demand is balanced where the source allows it.\n- [ ] Previous performance can inform future Interactive exams when available.\n- [ ] Randomness is used only as a final variation mechanism.\n- [ ] Failed audits trigger targeted replacement rather than blind reshuffling.\n- [ ] Overall Exam uses its own cross-chapter blueprint.\n- [ ] Identification-only tab uses intelligent Identification selection.","lineStart":1987,"lineEnd":3673,"totalLines":3673,"truncated":false}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\.opencode\\skills\\interactive-comprehensive-exam-generator\\SKILL.md","time":{"start":1789885031802,"end":1789885031839}},"id":"prt_0bd7623d4001Gjy1wcPHK73eSn","sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","messageID":"msg_0bd7615e8001kQtEA8dcYgK6iV"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885031982,"sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_cf62083b9d3148b9b68c5c93","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\worker.php"},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\worker.php</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: <?php\n2: declare(strict_types=1);\n3: \n4: require_once __DIR__ . '/lib/db.php';\n5: \n6: error_reporting(E_ALL);\n7: set_time_limit(0);\n8: \n9: if (PHP_SAPI !== 'cli') {\n10:     http_response_code(403);\n11:     exit('CLI only');\n12: }\n13: \n14: $id = preg_replace('/[^A-Za-z0-9\\-_]/', '', $argv[1] ?? '');\n15: register_shutdown_function(static function () use ($id): void {\n16:     if ($id !== '') {\n17:         remove_launch_task($id);\n18:     }\n19: });\n20: $dir = $id !== '' ? exam_dir($id) : '';\n21: \n22: if ($id === '' || !is_dir($dir)) {\n23:     fwrite(STDERR, \"Job not found: $id\\n\");\n24:     exit(1);\n25: }\n26: \n27: $job = json_decode((string)file_get_contents($dir . '/job.json'), true);\n28: if (!is_array($job)) {\n29:     fwrite(STDERR, \"No job metadata\\n\");\n30:     exit(1);\n31: }\n32: \n33: $statusFile = $job['status_file'] ?? $dir . '/status.json';\n34: $logFile = $job['log'] ?? $dir . '/opencode.log';\n35: $outFile = $job['output'] ?? $dir . '/questions.json';\n36: \n37: function set_job_status(string $statusFile, string $status, string $message, string $error = ''): void\n38: {\n39:     file_put_contents($statusFile, json_encode([\n40:         'status' => $status,\n41:         'message' => $message,\n42:         'error' => $error,\n43:         'updated' => date('c'),\n44:     ], JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE));\n45: }\n46: \n47: function remove_launch_task(string $id): void\n48: {\n49:     $taskName = 'autoExam_' . $id;\n50:     $proc = @proc_open('schtasks /Delete /TN ' . escapeshellarg($taskName) . ' /F', [1 => ['pipe', 'w'], 2 => ['pipe', 'w']], $pipes);\n51:     if (is_resource($proc)) {\n52:         while (true) {\n53:             stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n54:             stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n55:             $status = proc_get_status($proc);\n56:             if (!$status['running']) {\n57:                 break;\n58:             }\n59:             usleep(100000);\n60:         }\n61:         fclose($pipes[1]);\n62:         fclose($pipes[2]);\n63:         proc_close($proc);\n64:     }\n65:     @unlink(__DIR__ . '/data/.launch_' . $id . '.ps1');\n66: }\n67: \n68: $pdf = $job['pdf'] ?? null;\n69: if (!$pdf || !is_file($pdf)) {\n70:     set_job_status($statusFile, 'error', 'Source PDF missing.', 'Source PDF missing.');\n71:     exam_update($id, ['status' => 'error']);\n72:     exit(1);\n73: }\n74: \n75: set_job_status($statusFile, 'running', 'Generating exam');\n76: exam_update($id, ['status' => 'running']);\n77: app_log(\"Job $id: worker started\");\n78: \n79: $textFile = $dir . '/source.txt';\n80: $extracted = extract_pdf_text($pdf, $textFile);\n81: $sourceForModel = $extracted ? $textFile : $pdf;\n82: if ($extracted) {\n83:     app_log(\"Job $id: extracted PDF text -> \" . filesize($textFile) . \" bytes\");\n84: } else {\n85:     app_log(\"Job $id: WARNING - PDF text extraction failed, using PDF directly\");\n86: }\n87: \n88: $prompt = build_prompt($job, $textFile, $pdf);\n89: \n90: $exe = OPENCODE_EXE;\n91: $workDirArg = '--dir ' . escapeshellarg(str_replace('\\\\', '/', APP_ROOT));\n92: $fileArg = '--file ' . escapeshellarg($sourceForModel);\n93: $title = $job['title'] ?? 'Exam generation';\n94: $titleArg = '--title ' . escapeshellarg($title);\n95: $promptArg = escapeshellarg($prompt);\n96: \n97: $cmd = escapeshellarg($exe) . ' run --format json --auto '\n98:     . $workDirArg . ' ' . $fileArg . ' ' . $titleArg . ' ' . $promptArg . ' 2>&1';\n99: \n100: app_log(\"Job $id: running opencode command\");\n101: $cmdRender = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $cmd);\n102: file_put_contents($logFile, \"COMMAND: $cmdRender\\n\\n\", FILE_APPEND);\n103: \n104: $logHandle = fopen($logFile, 'a');\n105: if ($logHandle === false) {\n106:     $logHandle = tmpfile();\n107: }\n108: \n109: $output = [];\n110: $exitCode = 0;\n111: $isRunning = true;\n112: \n113: if (!is_null($logHandle)) {\n114:     $proc = proc_open($cmd, [1 => ['pipe', 'w'], 2 => ['pipe', 'w']], $pipes);\n115:     if (is_resource($proc)) {\n116:         stream_set_blocking($pipes[1], false);\n117:         stream_set_blocking($pipes[2], false);\n118:         while ($isRunning) {\n119:             $out = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n120:             $err = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n121:             if ($out !== '' && $out !== false) {\n122:                 fwrite($logHandle, $out);\n123:                 $output[] = $out;\n124:             }\n125:             if ($err !== '' && $err !== false) {\n126:                 fwrite($logHandle, $err);\n127:                 $output[] = $err;\n128:             }\n129:             $status = proc_get_status($proc);\n130:             if (!$status['running']) {\n131:                 $isRunning = false;\n132:                 // drain any remaining output\n133:                 $out = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n134:                 $err = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n135:                 if ($out) { fwrite($logHandle, $out); $output[] = $out; }\n136:                 if ($err) { fwrite($logHandle, $err); $output[] = $err; }\n137:                 $exitCode = $status['exitcode'];\n138:             } else {\n139:                 usleep(200000);\n140:             }\n141:         }\n142:         fclose($pipes[1]);\n143:         fclose($pipes[2]);\n144:         proc_close($proc);\n145:     } else {\n146:         fwrite($logHandle, \"FATAL: could not start opencode\\n\");\n147:         fclose($logHandle);\n148:         set_job_status($statusFile, 'error', 'Could not start opencode.', 'Could not start opencode process.');\n149:         exam_update($id, ['status' => 'error']);\n150:         exit(1);\n151:     }\n152:     fclose($logHandle);\n153: }\n154: \n155: app_log(\"Job $id: opencode exited with code $exitCode\");\n156: \n157: $data = null;\n158: if (is_file($outFile)) {\n159:     $data = json_decode((string)file_get_contents($outFile), true);\n160:     if (!is_array($data)) {\n161:         app_log(\"Job $id: questions.json is not valid JSON, trying log fallback\");\n162:         $data = extract_json_fallback($output);\n163:     }\n164: }\n165: \n166: if (!is_array($data) || !validate_bank($data)) {\n167:     $tail = '';\n168:     if (is_file($logFile)) {\n169:         $lines = file($logFile, FILE_IGNORE_NEW_LINES | FILE_SKIP_EMPTY_LINES) ?: [];\n170:         $tail = implode(\"\\n\", array_slice($lines, -30));\n171:     }\n172:     file_put_contents(\n173:         $dir . '/status.json',\n174:         json_encode([\n175:             'status' => 'error',\n176:             'message' => 'No valid question bank was produced.',\n177:             'error' => 'opencode exited but produced no valid question bank JSON.',\n178:             'log_tail' => $tail,\n179:             'updated' => date('c'),\n180:         ], JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE)\n181:     );\n182:     exam_update($id, ['status' => 'error']);\n183:     app_log(\"Job $id: FAILED - no valid question bank\");\n184:     exit(1);\n185: }\n186: \n187: $data = normalize_bank($data);\n188: file_put_contents($outFile, json_encode($data, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT | JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE));\n189: \n190: $totals = bank_totals($data);\n191: exam_update($id, [\n192:     'status' => 'done',\n193:     'title' => $data['title'] ?? ($job['title'] ?? 'Exam'),\n194:     'subject' => $data['subject'] ?? '',\n195:     'total_questions' => $totals['questions'],\n196:     'num_chapters' => $totals['chapters'],\n197: ]);\n198: \n199: set_job_status($statusFile, 'done', 'Exam ready.', '');\n200: app_log(\"Job $id: DONE - \" . $totals['chapters'] . \" chapters, \" . $totals['questions'] . \" questions\");\n201: \n202: exit(0);\n203: \n204: function build_prompt(array $job, string $textFile, string $pdf): string\n205: {\n206:     $src = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $textFile);\n207:     $pdfPath = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $pdf);\n208:     $out = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $job['output']);\n209: \n210:     return <<<PROMPT\n211: You are generating an interactive comprehensive exam system for a web app.\n212: \n213: STEP 1: Load the skill named \"interactive-comprehensive-exam-generator\" using your skill tool. Follow ALL of its instructions exactly (chapter organization of ~30 questions/chapter, timed/untimed modes, overall exam, answer key, encyclopedia, anti-guessing rules, balanced answers, stable question IDs, explanation for every question).\n214: \n215: STEP 2: The reference material is a plain-text extraction of the original PDF, located at:\n216: $src\n217: \n218: Read it with your Read tool. It is the PRIMARY SOURCE OF TRUTH. Do not invent facts unsupported by it. Identify its chapters/lessons and build a coverage plan, then generate the question bank.\n219: \n220: If the text extraction is empty, truncated, or unreadable, use the original PDF for reference instead:\n221: $pdfPath\n222: \n223: STEP 3: Write the question bank as ONE valid JSON object EXACTLY to this file:\n224: $out\n225: \n226: Do NOT create any HTML, CSS, JS, markdown, or other files. Do NOT print the JSON in your final reply; just write the file.\n227: \n228: Use EXACTLY this JSON schema:\n229: \n230: {\n231:   \"title\": \"Full exam title\",\n232:   \"subject\": \"Subject or course name\",\n233:   \"summary\": \"One short paragraph about coverage\",\n234:   \"chapters\": [\n235:     {\n236:       \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n237:       \"title\": \"Name of chapter/lesson 1\",\n238:       \"description\": \"Short description of this chapter\",\n239:       \"questions\": [\n240:         {\n241:           \"id\": \"ch1-q01\",\n242:           \"question\": \"The question text. Use scenarios, comparisons, exceptions, application and analysis, not just recall.\",\n243:           \"choices\": [\"Choice A text\", \"Choice B text\", \"Choice C text\", \"Choice D text\"],\n244:           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n245:           \"explanation\": \"Concise explanation of why this is correct and why the others are not.\"\n246:         }\n247:       ]\n248:     }\n249:   ],\n250:   \"encyclopedia\": [\n251:     { \"chapterId\": \"ch1\", \"term\": \"Important term\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n252:   ]\n253: }\n254: \n255: HARD RULES for the JSON:\n256: - correctAnswer is the 0-based INDEX of the correct choice within the choices array.\n257: - Every question MUST have exactly 4 choices and exactly one correct answer.\n258: - Every question MUST have id, question, choices, correctAnswer, explanation.\n259: - Every chapter MUST have chapterId, title, description, and at least 1 question.\n260: - Approximately 30 questions per chapter (fewer if the chapter is thin, never fabricate).\n261: - Balanced A/B/C/D correct-answer positions overall; no obvious answer patterns.\n262: - All-choice parity: correct answer must not be the longest/most technical/most specific option.\n263: - Explanations must be grounded in the reference.\n264: - encyclopedia MUST contain the important terms from the reference, at least 10 entries unless the reference is very small.\n265: - The JSON MUST be valid. Check it before writing.\n266: \n267: After writing the file, reply with a one-line confirmation telling me the exact path of the JSON file you wrote.\n268: PROMPT;\n269: }\n270: \n271: function extract_pdf_text(string $pdf, string $out): bool\n272: {\n273:     $script = <<<'PY'\n274: import sys\n275: try:\n276:     import pymupdf\n277: except ImportError:\n278:     try:\n279:         import fitz as pymupdf\n280:     except ImportError:\n281:         sys.exit(3)\n282: pdf = sys.argv[1]\n283: out = sys.argv[2].encode('utf-8')\n284: try:\n285:     doc = pymupdf.open(pdf)\n286: except Exception:\n287:     sys.exit(2)\n288: parts = []\n289: for i, page in enumerate(doc, 1):\n290:     parts.append(\"--- PAGE %d ---\" % i)\n291:     parts.append(page.get_text(\"text\") or \"\")\n292: text = \"\\n\".join(parts)\n293: if len(text.strip()) < 50:\n294:     sys.exit(4)\n295: with open(sys.argv[2], \"wb\") as f:\n296:     f.write(out + b\"\\n\" + text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n297: sys.exit(0)\n298: PY;\n299: \n300:     $tmp = @tempnam(sys_get_temp_dir(), 'pdf_extract_');\n301:     if ($tmp === false) {\n302:         app_log('PDF text extraction: could not create temp script file');\n303:         return false;\n304:     }\n305:     $scriptFile = $tmp . '.py';\n306:     if (!@rename($tmp, $scriptFile)) {\n307:         @unlink($tmp);\n308:         app_log('PDF text extraction: could not create temp script file');\n309:         return false;\n310:     }\n311:     if (@file_put_contents($scriptFile, $script) === false) {\n312:         @unlink($scriptFile);\n313:         app_log('PDF text extraction: could not write temp script file');\n314:         return false;\n315:     }\n316: \n317:     $cmd = escapeshellarg(PYTHON_EXE)\n318:         . ' ' . escapeshellarg($scriptFile)\n319:         . ' ' . escapeshellarg($pdf)\n320:         . ' ' . escapeshellarg($out)\n321:         . ' 2>&1';\n322: \n323:     $proc = @proc_open($cmd, [1 => ['pipe', 'w'], 2 => ['pipe', 'w']], $pipes);\n324:     if (!is_resource($proc)) {\n325:         @unlink($scriptFile);\n326:         app_log('PDF text extraction: could not start python (' . PYTHON_EXE . ')');\n327:         return false;\n328:     }\n329:     $err = '';\n330:     while (true) {\n331:         $o = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n332:         $e = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n333:         if ($o !== '' && $o !== false) { $err .= $o; }\n334:         if ($e !== '' && $e !== false) { $err .= $e; }\n335:         $status = proc_get_status($proc);\n336:         if (!$status['running']) {\n337:             $o = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n338:             $e = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n339:             if ($o) { $err .= $o; }\n340:             if ($e) { $err .= $e; }\n341:             break;\n342:         }\n343:         usleep(100000);\n344:     }\n345:     fclose($pipes[1]);\n346:     fclose($pipes[2]);\n347:     $exit = proc_close($proc);\n348:     @unlink($scriptFile);\n349:     if ($exit !== 0) {\n350:         app_log(\"PDF text extraction exited $exit: \" . substr($err, 0, 300));\n351:         return false;\n352:     }\n353:     return is_file($out) && filesize($out) > 50;\n354: }\n355: \n356: function extract_json_fallback(array $chunks): ?array\n357: {\n358:     $log = implode('', $chunks);\n359:     // find the last balanced { ... } block that looks like a question bank\n360:     $start = strrpos($log, '{');\n361:     if ($start === false) {\n362:         return null;\n363:     }\n364:     // naive brace matching from the last opening brace\n365:     $depth = 0;\n366:     $inStr = false;\n367:     $esc = false;\n368:     $end = null;\n369:     $len = strlen($log);\n370:     for ($i = $start; $i < $len; $i++) {\n371:         $c = $log[$i];\n372:         if ($inStr) {\n373:             if ($esc) {\n374:                 $esc = false;\n375:             } elseif ($c === '\\\\') {\n376:                 $esc = true;\n377:             } elseif ($c === '\"') {\n378:                 $inStr = false;\n379:             }\n380:             continue;\n381:         }\n382:         if ($c === '\"') {\n383:             $inStr = true;\n384:         } elseif ($c === '{') {\n385:             $depth++;\n386:         } elseif ($c === '}') {\n387:             $depth--;\n388:             if ($depth === 0) {\n389:                 $end = $i + 1;\n390:                 break;\n391:             }\n392:         }\n393:     }\n394:     if ($end === null) {\n395:         return null;\n396:     }\n397:     $candidate = substr($log, $start, $end - $start);\n398:     if (strlen($candidate) > 5 * 1024 * 1024) {\n399:         return null;\n400:     }\n401:     $decoded = json_decode($candidate, true);\n402:     return is_array($decoded) ? $decoded : null;\n403: }\n404: \n405: function validate_bank(array $data): bool\n406: {\n407:     if (empty($data['chapters']) || !is_array($data['chapters'])) {\n408:         return false;\n409:     }\n410:     $qCount = 0;\n411:     foreach ($data['chapters'] as $ch) {\n412:         if (!is_array($ch) || empty($ch['questions']) || !is_array($ch['questions'])) {\n413:             return false;\n414:         }\n415:         foreach ($ch['questions'] as $q) {\n416:             $qCount++;\n417:             if (!is_array($q)) {\n418:                 return false;\n419:             }\n420:             if (empty($q['question']) || empty($q['choices']) || count($q['choices']) !== 4) {\n421:                 return false;\n422:             }\n423:             if (!isset($q['correctAnswer']) || !is_int($q['correctAnswer']) || $q['correctAnswer'] < 0 || $q['correctAnswer'] > 3) {\n424:                 return false;\n425:             }\n426:             if (empty($q['explanation'])) {\n427:                 return false;\n428:             }\n429:         }\n430:     }\n431:     return $qCount >= 1;\n432: }\n433: \n434: function normalize_bank(array $data): array\n435: {\n436:     // keep only the fields our exam app consumes\n437:     return [\n438:         'title' => (string)($data['title'] ?? 'Untitled Exam'),\n439:         'subject' => (string)($data['subject'] ?? ''),\n440:         'summary' => (string)($data['summary'] ?? ''),\n441:         'chapters' => array_values(array_map(static function (array $ch, int $i): array {\n442:             return [\n443:                 'chapterId' => (string)($ch['chapterId'] ?? 'ch' . ($i + 1)),\n444:                 'title' => (string)($ch['title'] ?? 'Chapter ' . ($i + 1)),\n445:                 'description' => (string)($ch['description'] ?? ''),\n446:                 'questions' => array_values(array_map(static function (array $q): array {\n447:                     return [\n448:                         'id' => (string)($q['id'] ?? ''),\n449:                         'question' => (string)$q['question'],\n450:                         'choices' => array_values(array_map('strval', $q['choices'])),\n451:                         'correctAnswer' => (int)$q['correctAnswer'],\n452:                         'explanation' => (string)$q['explanation'],\n453:                     ];\n454:                 }, $ch['questions'])),\n455:             ];\n456:         }, $data['chapters'], array_keys($data['chapters']))),\n457:         'encyclopedia' => array_values(array_map(static function (array $e): array {\n458:             return [\n459:                 'chapterId' => (string)($e['chapterId'] ?? ''),\n460:                 'term' => (string)($e['term'] ?? ''),\n461:                 'definition' => (string)($e['definition'] ?? ''),\n462:             ];\n463:         }, $data['encyclopedia'] ?? [])),\n464:     ];\n465: }\n466: \n467: function bank_totals(array $data): array\n468: {\n469:     $q = 0;\n470:     foreach ($data['chapters'] as $ch) {\n471:         $q += count($ch['questions']);\n472:     }\n473:     return ['chapters' => count($data['chapters']), 'questions' => $q];\n474: }\n\n(End of file - total 474 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"<?php\ndeclare(strict_types=1);\n\nrequire_once __DIR__ . '/lib/db.php';\n\nerror_reporting(E_ALL);\nset_time_limit(0);\n\nif (PHP_SAPI !== 'cli') {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('CLI only');\n}\n\n$id = preg_replace('/[^A-Za-z0-9\\-_]/', '', $argv[1] ?? '');\nregister_shutdown_function(static function () use ($id): void {\n    if ($id !== '') {\n        remove_launch_task($id);\n    }\n});\n$dir = $id !== '' ? exam_dir($id) : '';","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\worker.php","text":"<?php\ndeclare(strict_types=1);\n\nrequire_once __DIR__ . '/lib/db.php';\n\nerror_reporting(E_ALL);\nset_time_limit(0);\n\nif (PHP_SAPI !== 'cli') {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('CLI only');\n}\n\n$id = preg_replace('/[^A-Za-z0-9\\-_]/', '', $argv[1] ?? '');\nregister_shutdown_function(static function () use ($id): void {\n    if ($id !== '') {\n        remove_launch_task($id);\n    }\n});\n$dir = $id !== '' ? exam_dir($id) : '';\n\nif ($id === '' || !is_dir($dir)) {\n    fwrite(STDERR, \"Job not found: $id\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n$job = json_decode((string)file_get_contents($dir . '/job.json'), true);\nif (!is_array($job)) {\n    fwrite(STDERR, \"No job metadata\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n$statusFile = $job['status_file'] ?? $dir . '/status.json';\n$logFile = $job['log'] ?? $dir . '/opencode.log';\n$outFile = $job['output'] ?? $dir . '/questions.json';\n\nfunction set_job_status(string $statusFile, string $status, string $message, string $error = ''): void\n{\n    file_put_contents($statusFile, json_encode([\n        'status' => $status,\n        'message' => $message,\n        'error' => $error,\n        'updated' => date('c'),\n    ], JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE));\n}\n\nfunction remove_launch_task(string $id): void\n{\n    $taskName = 'autoExam_' . $id;\n    $proc = @proc_open('schtasks /Delete /TN ' . escapeshellarg($taskName) . ' /F', [1 => ['pipe', 'w'], 2 => ['pipe', 'w']], $pipes);\n    if (is_resource($proc)) {\n        while (true) {\n            stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n            stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n            $status = proc_get_status($proc);\n            if (!$status['running']) {\n                break;\n            }\n            usleep(100000);\n        }\n        fclose($pipes[1]);\n        fclose($pipes[2]);\n        proc_close($proc);\n    }\n    @unlink(__DIR__ . '/data/.launch_' . $id . '.ps1');\n}\n\n$pdf = $job['pdf'] ?? null;\nif (!$pdf || !is_file($pdf)) {\n    set_job_status($statusFile, 'error', 'Source PDF missing.', 'Source PDF missing.');\n    exam_update($id, ['status' => 'error']);\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nset_job_status($statusFile, 'running', 'Generating exam');\nexam_update($id, ['status' => 'running']);\napp_log(\"Job $id: worker started\");\n\n$textFile = $dir . '/source.txt';\n$extracted = extract_pdf_text($pdf, $textFile);\n$sourceForModel = $extracted ? $textFile : $pdf;\nif ($extracted) {\n    app_log(\"Job $id: extracted PDF text -> \" . filesize($textFile) . \" bytes\");\n} else {\n    app_log(\"Job $id: WARNING - PDF text extraction failed, using PDF directly\");\n}\n\n$prompt = build_prompt($job, $textFile, $pdf);\n\n$exe = OPENCODE_EXE;\n$workDirArg = '--dir ' . escapeshellarg(str_replace('\\\\', '/', APP_ROOT));\n$fileArg = '--file ' . escapeshellarg($sourceForModel);\n$title = $job['title'] ?? 'Exam generation';\n$titleArg = '--title ' . escapeshellarg($title);\n$promptArg = escapeshellarg($prompt);\n\n$cmd = escapeshellarg($exe) . ' run --format json --auto '\n    . $workDirArg . ' ' . $fileArg . ' ' . $titleArg . ' ' . $promptArg . ' 2>&1';\n\napp_log(\"Job $id: running opencode command\");\n$cmdRender = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $cmd);\nfile_put_contents($logFile, \"COMMAND: $cmdRender\\n\\n\", FILE_APPEND);\n\n$logHandle = fopen($logFile, 'a');\nif ($logHandle === false) {\n    $logHandle = tmpfile();\n}\n\n$output = [];\n$exitCode = 0;\n$isRunning = true;\n\nif (!is_null($logHandle)) {\n    $proc = proc_open($cmd, [1 => ['pipe', 'w'], 2 => ['pipe', 'w']], $pipes);\n    if (is_resource($proc)) {\n        stream_set_blocking($pipes[1], false);\n        stream_set_blocking($pipes[2], false);\n        while ($isRunning) {\n            $out = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n            $err = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n            if ($out !== '' && $out !== false) {\n                fwrite($logHandle, $out);\n                $output[] = $out;\n            }\n            if ($err !== '' && $err !== false) {\n                fwrite($logHandle, $err);\n                $output[] = $err;\n            }\n            $status = proc_get_status($proc);\n            if (!$status['running']) {\n                $isRunning = false;\n                // drain any remaining output\n                $out = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n                $err = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n                if ($out) { fwrite($logHandle, $out); $output[] = $out; }\n                if ($err) { fwrite($logHandle, $err); $output[] = $err; }\n                $exitCode = $status['exitcode'];\n            } else {\n                usleep(200000);\n            }\n        }\n        fclose($pipes[1]);\n        fclose($pipes[2]);\n        proc_close($proc);\n    } else {\n        fwrite($logHandle, \"FATAL: could not start opencode\\n\");\n        fclose($logHandle);\n        set_job_status($statusFile, 'error', 'Could not start opencode.', 'Could not start opencode process.');\n        exam_update($id, ['status' => 'error']);\n        exit(1);\n    }\n    fclose($logHandle);\n}\n\napp_log(\"Job $id: opencode exited with code $exitCode\");\n\n$data = null;\nif (is_file($outFile)) {\n    $data = json_decode((string)file_get_contents($outFile), true);\n    if (!is_array($data)) {\n        app_log(\"Job $id: questions.json is not valid JSON, trying log fallback\");\n        $data = extract_json_fallback($output);\n    }\n}\n\nif (!is_array($data) || !validate_bank($data)) {\n    $tail = '';\n    if (is_file($logFile)) {\n        $lines = file($logFile, FILE_IGNORE_NEW_LINES | FILE_SKIP_EMPTY_LINES) ?: [];\n        $tail = implode(\"\\n\", array_slice($lines, -30));\n    }\n    file_put_contents(\n        $dir . '/status.json',\n        json_encode([\n            'status' => 'error',\n            'message' => 'No valid question bank was produced.',\n            'error' => 'opencode exited but produced no valid question bank JSON.',\n            'log_tail' => $tail,\n            'updated' => date('c'),\n        ], JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE)\n    );\n    exam_update($id, ['status' => 'error']);\n    app_log(\"Job $id: FAILED - no valid question bank\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n$data = normalize_bank($data);\nfile_put_contents($outFile, json_encode($data, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT | JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE));\n\n$totals = bank_totals($data);\nexam_update($id, [\n    'status' => 'done',\n    'title' => $data['title'] ?? ($job['title'] ?? 'Exam'),\n    'subject' => $data['subject'] ?? '',\n    'total_questions' => $totals['questions'],\n    'num_chapters' => $totals['chapters'],\n]);\n\nset_job_status($statusFile, 'done', 'Exam ready.', '');\napp_log(\"Job $id: DONE - \" . $totals['chapters'] . \" chapters, \" . $totals['questions'] . \" questions\");\n\nexit(0);\n\nfunction build_prompt(array $job, string $textFile, string $pdf): string\n{\n    $src = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $textFile);\n    $pdfPath = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $pdf);\n    $out = str_replace('\\\\', '/', $job['output']);\n\n    return <<<PROMPT\nYou are generating an interactive comprehensive exam system for a web app.\n\nSTEP 1: Load the skill named \"interactive-comprehensive-exam-generator\" using your skill tool. Follow ALL of its instructions exactly (chapter organization of ~30 questions/chapter, timed/untimed modes, overall exam, answer key, encyclopedia, anti-guessing rules, balanced answers, stable question IDs, explanation for every question).\n\nSTEP 2: The reference material is a plain-text extraction of the original PDF, located at:\n$src\n\nRead it with your Read tool. It is the PRIMARY SOURCE OF TRUTH. Do not invent facts unsupported by it. Identify its chapters/lessons and build a coverage plan, then generate the question bank.\n\nIf the text extraction is empty, truncated, or unreadable, use the original PDF for reference instead:\n$pdfPath\n\nSTEP 3: Write the question bank as ONE valid JSON object EXACTLY to this file:\n$out\n\nDo NOT create any HTML, CSS, JS, markdown, or other files. Do NOT print the JSON in your final reply; just write the file.\n\nUse EXACTLY this JSON schema:\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"Full exam title\",\n  \"subject\": \"Subject or course name\",\n  \"summary\": \"One short paragraph about coverage\",\n  \"chapters\": [\n    {\n      \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n      \"title\": \"Name of chapter/lesson 1\",\n      \"description\": \"Short description of this chapter\",\n      \"questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ch1-q01\",\n          \"question\": \"The question text. Use scenarios, comparisons, exceptions, application and analysis, not just recall.\",\n          \"choices\": [\"Choice A text\", \"Choice B text\", \"Choice C text\", \"Choice D text\"],\n          \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n          \"explanation\": \"Concise explanation of why this is correct and why the others are not.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"encyclopedia\": [\n    { \"chapterId\": \"ch1\", \"term\": \"Important term\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n  ]\n}\n\nHARD RULES for the JSON:\n- correctAnswer is the 0-based INDEX of the correct choice within the choices array.\n- Every question MUST have exactly 4 choices and exactly one correct answer.\n- Every question MUST have id, question, choices, correctAnswer, explanation.\n- Every chapter MUST have chapterId, title, description, and at least 1 question.\n- Approximately 30 questions per chapter (fewer if the chapter is thin, never fabricate).\n- Balanced A/B/C/D correct-answer positions overall; no obvious answer patterns.\n- All-choice parity: correct answer must not be the longest/most technical/most specific option.\n- Explanations must be grounded in the reference.\n- encyclopedia MUST contain the important terms from the reference, at least 10 entries unless the reference is very small.\n- The JSON MUST be valid. Check it before writing.\n\nAfter writing the file, reply with a one-line confirmation telling me the exact path of the JSON file you wrote.\nPROMPT;\n}\n\nfunction extract_pdf_text(string $pdf, string $out): bool\n{\n    $script = <<<'PY'\nimport sys\ntry:\n    import pymupdf\nexcept ImportError:\n    try:\n        import fitz as pymupdf\n    except ImportError:\n        sys.exit(3)\npdf = sys.argv[1]\nout = sys.argv[2].encode('utf-8')\ntry:\n    doc = pymupdf.open(pdf)\nexcept Exception:\n    sys.exit(2)\nparts = []\nfor i, page in enumerate(doc, 1):\n    parts.append(\"--- PAGE %d ---\" % i)\n    parts.append(page.get_text(\"text\") or \"\")\ntext = \"\\n\".join(parts)\nif len(text.strip()) < 50:\n    sys.exit(4)\nwith open(sys.argv[2], \"wb\") as f:\n    f.write(out + b\"\\n\" + text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\nsys.exit(0)\nPY;\n\n    $tmp = @tempnam(sys_get_temp_dir(), 'pdf_extract_');\n    if ($tmp === false) {\n        app_log('PDF text extraction: could not create temp script file');\n        return false;\n    }\n    $scriptFile = $tmp . '.py';\n    if (!@rename($tmp, $scriptFile)) {\n        @unlink($tmp);\n        app_log('PDF text extraction: could not create temp script file');\n        return false;\n    }\n    if (@file_put_contents($scriptFile, $script) === false) {\n        @unlink($scriptFile);\n        app_log('PDF text extraction: could not write temp script file');\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    $cmd = escapeshellarg(PYTHON_EXE)\n        . ' ' . escapeshellarg($scriptFile)\n        . ' ' . escapeshellarg($pdf)\n        . ' ' . escapeshellarg($out)\n        . ' 2>&1';\n\n    $proc = @proc_open($cmd, [1 => ['pipe', 'w'], 2 => ['pipe', 'w']], $pipes);\n    if (!is_resource($proc)) {\n        @unlink($scriptFile);\n        app_log('PDF text extraction: could not start python (' . PYTHON_EXE . ')');\n        return false;\n    }\n    $err = '';\n    while (true) {\n        $o = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n        $e = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n        if ($o !== '' && $o !== false) { $err .= $o; }\n        if ($e !== '' && $e !== false) { $err .= $e; }\n        $status = proc_get_status($proc);\n        if (!$status['running']) {\n            $o = stream_get_contents($pipes[1]);\n            $e = stream_get_contents($pipes[2]);\n            if ($o) { $err .= $o; }\n            if ($e) { $err .= $e; }\n            break;\n        }\n        usleep(100000);\n    }\n    fclose($pipes[1]);\n    fclose($pipes[2]);\n    $exit = proc_close($proc);\n    @unlink($scriptFile);\n    if ($exit !== 0) {\n        app_log(\"PDF text extraction exited $exit: \" . substr($err, 0, 300));\n        return false;\n    }\n    return is_file($out) && filesize($out) > 50;\n}\n\nfunction extract_json_fallback(array $chunks): ?array\n{\n    $log = implode('', $chunks);\n    // find the last balanced { ... } block that looks like a question bank\n    $start = strrpos($log, '{');\n    if ($start === false) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    // naive brace matching from the last opening brace\n    $depth = 0;\n    $inStr = false;\n    $esc = false;\n    $end = null;\n    $len = strlen($log);\n    for ($i = $start; $i < $len; $i++) {\n        $c = $log[$i];\n        if ($inStr) {\n            if ($esc) {\n                $esc = false;\n            } elseif ($c === '\\\\') {\n                $esc = true;\n            } elseif ($c === '\"') {\n                $inStr = false;\n            }\n            continue;\n        }\n        if ($c === '\"') {\n            $inStr = true;\n        } elseif ($c === '{') {\n            $depth++;\n        } elseif ($c === '}') {\n            $depth--;\n            if ($depth === 0) {\n                $end = $i + 1;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    if ($end === null) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    $candidate = substr($log, $start, $end - $start);\n    if (strlen($candidate) > 5 * 1024 * 1024) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    $decoded = json_decode($candidate, true);\n    return is_array($decoded) ? $decoded : null;\n}\n\nfunction validate_bank(array $data): bool\n{\n    if (empty($data['chapters']) || !is_array($data['chapters'])) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    $qCount = 0;\n    foreach ($data['chapters'] as $ch) {\n        if (!is_array($ch) || empty($ch['questions']) || !is_array($ch['questions'])) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        foreach ($ch['questions'] as $q) {\n            $qCount++;\n            if (!is_array($q)) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (empty($q['question']) || empty($q['choices']) || count($q['choices']) !== 4) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (!isset($q['correctAnswer']) || !is_int($q['correctAnswer']) || $q['correctAnswer'] < 0 || $q['correctAnswer'] > 3) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (empty($q['explanation'])) {\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return $qCount >= 1;\n}\n\nfunction normalize_bank(array $data): array\n{\n    // keep only the fields our exam app consumes\n    return [\n        'title' => (string)($data['title'] ?? 'Untitled Exam'),\n        'subject' => (string)($data['subject'] ?? ''),\n        'summary' => (string)($data['summary'] ?? ''),\n        'chapters' => array_values(array_map(static function (array $ch, int $i): array {\n            return [\n                'chapterId' => (string)($ch['chapterId'] ?? 'ch' . ($i + 1)),\n                'title' => (string)($ch['title'] ?? 'Chapter ' . ($i + 1)),\n                'description' => (string)($ch['description'] ?? ''),\n                'questions' => array_values(array_map(static function (array $q): array {\n                    return [\n                        'id' => (string)($q['id'] ?? ''),\n                        'question' => (string)$q['question'],\n                        'choices' => array_values(array_map('strval', $q['choices'])),\n                        'correctAnswer' => (int)$q['correctAnswer'],\n                        'explanation' => (string)$q['explanation'],\n                    ];\n                }, $ch['questions'])),\n            ];\n        }, $data['chapters'], array_keys($data['chapters']))),\n        'encyclopedia' => array_values(array_map(static function (array $e): array {\n            return [\n                'chapterId' => (string)($e['chapterId'] ?? ''),\n                'term' => (string)($e['term'] ?? ''),\n                'definition' => (string)($e['definition'] ?? ''),\n            ];\n        }, $data['encyclopedia'] ?? [])),\n    ];\n}\n\nfunction bank_totals(array $data): array\n{\n    $q = 0;\n    foreach ($data['chapters'] as $ch) {\n        $q += count($ch['questions']);\n    }\n    return ['chapters' => count($data['chapters']), 'questions' => $q];\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":474,"totalLines":474,"truncated":false}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\worker.php","time":{"start":1789885031970,"end":1789885031979}},"id":"prt_0bd762589001i0lrUluvE6Rmsg","sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","messageID":"msg_0bd7615e8001kQtEA8dcYgK6iV"}}
