{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885504062,"sessionID":"ses_f428a512fffed89OwSRaaEXqP0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_2a73936f77844bcc872603b2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\29l6wh2f5izows\\questions.json","content":"{\n    \"title\": \"Linux System Administration Comprehensive Exam\",\n    \"subject\": \"System Administration and Maintenance (Linux)\",\n    \"summary\": \"Interactive examination covering five lessons: System Administration and Maintenance (Linux) with operating system comparisons, Linux-versus-Windows differences, the kernel, shells and shell prompts; the File System with Linux and Windows file systems, NFS, ZFS, and the Linux directory structure; Linux distributions and their release timeline; desktop environments, package management systems, boot loaders, and installation; and basic commands covering paths, directories, files, users and groups, filters, archiving, utilities, networking, and links.\",\n    \"chapters\": [\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"title\": \"System Administration and Maintenance (Linux)\",\n            \"description\": \"Operating system comparison pros and cons, fundamental Linux-versus-Windows differences, mistakes of new Linux users, kernel objectives, kernel functions and management, kernel types, and shell types with their prompts.\",\n            \"questions\": [\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q01\",\n                    \"question\": \"In the abbreviation LAMP, what does the letter M stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"MySQL\",\n                        \"Microsoft\",\n                        \"Memory\",\n                        \"Module\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"LAMP stands for Linux, Apache, MySQL, Perl/PHP/Python, so M is MySQL.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q02\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these stands for the Free Software Foundation?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"FST\",\n                        \"FSF\",\n                        \"FSS\",\n                        \"FRS\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"FSF is the Free Software Foundation, a supporter of the GNU project.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q03\",\n                    \"question\": \"POSIX is an abbreviation for which standard interface?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Portable Operating System Interface\",\n                        \"Primary Operating System Interface\",\n                        \"Post-Operating System Interface\",\n                        \"Protocol Operating System Interface\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"POSIX is the Portable Operating System Interface (Single UNIX Specification).\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q04\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of the following is listed as a Windows con in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Fewer games and software\",\n                        \"No hardware customization\",\n                        \"Virus attacks\",\n                        \"Steep learning curve\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Virus attacks are listed among Windows cons; the other choices are Mac or Linux cons.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q05\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which advantage is attributed to macOS in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Support for all hardware\",\n                        \"Fewer virus attacks\",\n                        \"No need for anti-virus\",\n                        \"Low system specifications\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"For Mac, the reference lists fewer virus attacks as one of its pros.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q06\",\n                    \"question\": \"What qualification does the reference add after stating Linux needs no anti-virus?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"No OS is totally secure\",\n                        \"Only paid editions remain safe\",\n                        \"Windows is fully secure\",\n                        \"Firewalls are unnecessary\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Linux does not need anti-virus, but the reference adds that no OS is totally secure.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q07\",\n                    \"question\": \"According to the reference, what does Linux need in order to execute code?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"A licensed kernel\",\n                        \"Root privileges\",\n                        \"An anti-virus scan\",\n                        \"A desktop environment\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Linux needs root privileges to be able to execute codes, as stated in the fundamental differences.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q08\",\n                    \"question\": \"How does Linux handle updates according to the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Updates everything at once and never restarts without permission\",\n                        \"Updates selected software and restarts without consent\",\n                        \"Requires a paid subscription per update\",\n                        \"Delays all updates until the next major release\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Linux updates everything at once and never restarts without permission, unlike the Windows behavior described.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q09\",\n                    \"question\": \"How do Linux and Windows differ in how they represent drives?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Windows uses alphabet letters; Linux uses /dev\",\n                        \"Linux uses alphabet letters; Windows uses /dev\",\n                        \"Both use mount points for every drive\",\n                        \"Both rely on network shares only\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Linux uses /dev for drives while Windows uses alphabet letters, per the reference.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q10\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is listed as a mistake made by new Linux users?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Reading the manual pages before installing\",\n                        \"Using Google to find software\",\n                        \"Choosing the recommended distribution\",\n                        \"Backing up files before partition changes\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Using Google to find software is among the mistakes new Linux users make.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q11\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which is one of the five kernel objectives in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Establish communication between user-level applications and hardware\",\n                        \"Render the graphical desktop for the user\",\n                        \"Compile source code into binary packages\",\n                        \"Store user documents and settings\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"A kernel objective is to establish communication between user-level applications and hardware, plus controlling disk, memory, and task management.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q12\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of the following is something the kernel manages?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Desktop wallpapers\",\n                        \"CPU and GPU\",\n                        \"Email accounts\",\n                        \"Browser bookmarks\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The kernel manages CPU/GPU, memory, I/O devices, resources, devices, system calls, and security and protection.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q13\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is NOT a kernel function named in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Process management\",\n                        \"Memory management\",\n                        \"Device management\",\n                        \"Web server configuration\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Kernel functions include process, memory, device, and file system management plus IPC and scheduling; web server configuration is not one.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q14\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a type of kernel listed in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Shell kernel\",\n                        \"Display kernel\",\n                        \"Microkernel\",\n                        \"Network kernel\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Microkernel is one of the listed kernel types, along with monolithic, hybrid, exo, nano, VM, and embedded kernels.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q15\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which kernel type is also referred to as a Virtual Machine (VM) kernel or hypervisor?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Nano Kernel\",\n                        \"Microkernel\",\n                        \"Exo Kernel\",\n                        \"VM Kernel or Hypervisor\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The VM Kernel or Hypervisor is one of the seven kernel types listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q16\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which shell is the GNU Bourne-Again Shell?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"bash\",\n                        \"csh\",\n                        \"ksh\",\n                        \"zsh\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"bash is the GNU Bourne-Again Shell.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q17\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which shell is described as the Friendly Interactive Shell?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"tcsh\",\n                        \"fish / fsh\",\n                        \"sh\",\n                        \"csh\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"fish (or fsh), the Friendly Interactive Shell, is one of the shell types listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q18\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which prompt character indicates a root user in the Bourne shell?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"$\",\n                        \"%\",\n                        \"#\",\n                        \">\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The Bourne shell shows # for root and $ for non-root users.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q19\",\n                    \"question\": \"What is the non-root prompt in the C shell (csh)?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"%\",\n                        \"#\",\n                        \"$\",\n                        \">\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The C shell uses # for root and % for non-root users.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q20\",\n                    \"question\": \"What is the root prompt in the Z Shell (zsh)?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"hostname>$\",\n                        \"hostname>#\",\n                        \"bash-VersionNumber#\",\n                        \"%\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The Z Shell shows hostname># for root and hostname>$ for non-root users.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q21\",\n                    \"question\": \"SCSI stands for which of the following?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Small Computer System Interface\",\n                        \"Serial Computer Storage Interface\",\n                        \"Standard Console System Interface\",\n                        \"System Control and Storage Input\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"SCSI is the Small Computer System Interface.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q22\",\n                    \"question\": \"ACPI is an abbreviation for which interface standard?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Advanced Control and Power Integration\",\n                        \"Advanced Configuration and Power Interface\",\n                        \"Automatic Configuration Protocol Interface\",\n                        \"Active Computing and Processor Index\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"ACPI is the Advanced Configuration and Power Interface.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q23\",\n                    \"question\": \"NUMA stands for which memory architecture?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Non-Uniform Memory Access\",\n                        \"Network Unified Memory Allocation\",\n                        \"Non-Volatile Unified Memory Array\",\n                        \"Node-Universal Memory Addressing\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"NUMA is Non-Uniform Memory Access.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q24\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does initramfs stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Initial Random Access Memory File Store\",\n                        \"Initial RAM File System\",\n                        \"Integrated RAM File Service\",\n                        \"Immediate Runtime Memory File System\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"initramfs is the Initial RAM File System.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q25\",\n                    \"question\": \"Btrfs stands for which of the following?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Backup Transfer File System\",\n                        \"Binary Tree File System\",\n                        \"B-tree File System\",\n                        \"Basic Transactional File System\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Btrfs is the B-tree File System.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q26\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does the statement 'Linux is yours, Windows is still theirs' mean in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Windows can only be copied after payment\",\n                        \"Linux gives the user control, while Windows remains controlled by its vendor\",\n                        \"Linux belongs to a single company\",\n                        \"Windows cannot be modified at all\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The reference contrasts Linux as user-controlled versus Windows as vendor-controlled software.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q27\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which two drawbacks are both listed under the Mac cons in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Expensive and fewer games and software\",\n                        \"Virus attacks and paid software\",\n                        \"Hardware drivers and learning curve\",\n                        \"High resources and technical support\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Under Mac cons the reference lists expensive, fewer games and software, and no hardware customization.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q28\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which item appears in the Linux pros list in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Support for all hardware\",\n                        \"Powerful CLI\",\n                        \"Similar GUI for all products\",\n                        \"Plug and play feature\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The Linux pros include open-source, no anti-virus need, powerful CLI, low specs, multitasking, and less disk space.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q29\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which kernel function deals with communication between running processes?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Inter-Process Communication\",\n                        \"Memory management\",\n                        \"Device management\",\n                        \"Task scheduling\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Inter-Process Communication (IPC) is a kernel function that handles process communication.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch1-q30\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does SELinux stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Secure Enterprise Linux\",\n                        \"Security-Enhanced Linux\",\n                        \"System Encryption Layer\",\n                        \"Standard Elevated Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"SELinux is Security-Enhanced Linux.\"\n                }\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n            \"title\": \"File System\",\n            \"description\": \"File system abbreviations, Linux and Microsoft Windows file systems, ZFS use cases, NFS components, and the Linux directory structure under the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard.\",\n            \"questions\": [\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q01\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which abbreviation stands for Filesystem Hierarchy Standard?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"FHS\",\n                        \"FSH\",\n                        \"FST\",\n                        \"FTS\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"FHS is the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard that defines Linux directory locations.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q02\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does COW stand for in file system terms?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Copy-on-Write\",\n                        \"Create-or-Write\",\n                        \"Copy-Over-Write\",\n                        \"Cache-on-Write\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"COW stands for Copy-on-Write.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q03\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is listed as a Linux file system in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"NTFS\",\n                        \"exFAT\",\n                        \"Btrfs\",\n                        \"ReFS\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Btrfs is listed among Linux file systems, while NTFS, exFAT, and ReFS are Windows file systems.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q04\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does the abbreviation XFS stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Extended File System\",\n                        \"X File System\",\n                        \"Xtra Fast System\",\n                        \"eXtensible File Store\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"XFS stands for X File System.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q05\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does ZFS stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Zettabyte File System\",\n                        \"Zero File System\",\n                        \"Zipped File System\",\n                        \"Zone File System\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"ZFS stands for Zettabyte File System.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q06\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does exFAT stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Extended Fast Allocation Table\",\n                        \"Extended File Allocation Table\",\n                        \"External File Access Table\",\n                        \"Extended Format Archive Table\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"exFAT is the Extended File Allocation Table.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q07\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does NTFS stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"New Technology File System\",\n                        \"Network Transfer File System\",\n                        \"Native Text File System\",\n                        \"Next-gen Table File System\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"NTFS is the New Technology File System.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q08\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does ReFS stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Recoverable File System\",\n                        \"Resilient File System\",\n                        \"Redundant File Store\",\n                        \"Runtime File System\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"ReFS is the Resilient File System.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q09\",\n                    \"question\": \"What are the two components of NFS named in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"NFS Server and NFS Client\",\n                        \"NFS Host and NFS Reader\",\n                        \"NFS Master and NFS Slave\",\n                        \"NFS Source and NFS Cache\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The components of NFS are the NFS Server and the NFS Client.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q10\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is listed as a common use case for ZFS?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Booting the operating system\",\n                        \"Backup and Disaster Recovery\",\n                        \"Compressing log files\",\n                        \"Serving web pages directly\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"ZFS use cases include Enterprise Storage Solutions, Backup and Disaster Recovery, and File Server and NAS.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q11\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a Microsoft Windows file system in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Minix\",\n                        \"ReiserFS\",\n                        \"Live File System\",\n                        \"JFS\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"MS Windows file systems are FAT, NTFS, exFAT, Live File System, and ReFS.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q12\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which Linux directory holds host-specific system-wide configuration files?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/etc\",\n                        \"/home\",\n                        \"/var\",\n                        \"/tmp\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/etc holds host-specific system-wide configuration files.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q13\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory contains user home directories?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/root\",\n                        \"/home\",\n                        \"/usr\",\n                        \"/srv\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/home contains user home directories.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q14\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory contains device files?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/usr\",\n                        \"/bin\",\n                        \"/dev\",\n                        \"/proc\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/dev is the device files directory.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q15\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory is a virtual filesystem for process and kernel information?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/proc\",\n                        \"/sys\",\n                        \"/run\",\n                        \"/opt\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/proc is the virtual filesystem for process and kernel information.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q16\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory stores variable data files?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/tmp\",\n                        \"/var\",\n                        \"/usr\",\n                        \"/srv\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/var holds variable data files.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q17\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory is intended for temporary files?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/tmp\",\n                        \"/run\",\n                        \"/cache\",\n                        \"/media\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/tmp is the temporary files directory.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q18\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory is the secondary hierarchy for user data and applications?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/usr\",\n                        \"/bin\",\n                        \"/var\",\n                        \"/home\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/usr is the secondary hierarchy for user data and applications.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q19\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory is the home directory for the root user?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/var\",\n                        \"/home\",\n                        \"/root\",\n                        \"/sbin\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/root is the home directory for the root user.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q20\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory provides mount points for removable media?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/mnt\",\n                        \"/media\",\n                        \"/dev\",\n                        \"/sys\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/media holds mount points for removable media.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q21\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory is used for temporarily mounted filesystems?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/mnt\",\n                        \"/media\",\n                        \"/opt\",\n                        \"/run\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/mnt is for temporarily mounted filesystems.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q22\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds essential system administrative binaries?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/sbin\",\n                        \"/bin\",\n                        \"/usr\",\n                        \"/sys\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/sbin holds essential system administrative binaries.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q23\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds add-on application software packages?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/opt\",\n                        \"/srv\",\n                        \"/lib\",\n                        \"/etc\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/opt holds add-on application software packages.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q24\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds site-specific data served by the system?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/srv\",\n                        \"/var\",\n                        \"/www\",\n                        \"/data\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/srv holds site-specific data served by the system.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q25\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds run-time variable data?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/run\",\n                        \"/tmp\",\n                        \"/proc\",\n                        \"/sys\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/run holds run-time variable data.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q26\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds device, driver, and kernel feature information?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/sys\",\n                        \"/proc\",\n                        \"/dev\",\n                        \"/etc\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/sys holds device, driver, and kernel feature information.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q27\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds essential command binaries?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/bin\",\n                        \"/sbin\",\n                        \"/opt\",\n                        \"/boot\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/bin holds essential command binaries.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q28\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which directory holds the boot loader files?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/boot\",\n                        \"/bin\",\n                        \"/dev\",\n                        \"/lib\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/boot holds boot loader files.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q29\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does the abbreviation USR stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"User Software Repository\",\n                        \"User System Resources\",\n                        \"Unified System Registry\",\n                        \"Universal Storage Region\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"USR stands for User System Resources.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch2-q30\",\n                    \"question\": \"In the Linux directory structure, which of these is the primary root directory written as a single slash?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"/\",\n                        \"/root\",\n                        \"/usr\",\n                        \"/home\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The primary root directory of the Linux hierarchy is /.\"\n                }\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch3\",\n            \"title\": \"Linux Distributions\",\n            \"description\": \"Linux distribution abbreviations and the release timeline of the distributions listed in the reference, from Slackware in July 1993 to Mageia in June 2011.\",\n            \"questions\": [\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q01\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does YaST stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Yet another Setup Tool\",\n                        \"Your System Test\",\n                        \"Yet another Storage Tool\",\n                        \"Young Support Tool\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"YaST is 'Yet another Setup Tool', the configuration tool used by SUSE.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q02\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does RHEL stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Red Hat Enterprise Linux\",\n                        \"Red Hat Extended Linux\",\n                        \"Rocky Hat Enhanced Linux\",\n                        \"Redcore Enterprise Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"RHEL is Red Hat Enterprise Linux.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q03\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does KVM stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Kernel Visual Machine\",\n                        \"Kernel-based Virtual Machine\",\n                        \"Keyed Virtual Memory\",\n                        \"Kernel Virtual Module\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"KVM is the Kernel-based Virtual Machine.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q04\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution in the reference timeline has the earliest release date?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Debian (September 1993)\",\n                        \"Red Hat (May 1995)\",\n                        \"Slackware (July 1993)\",\n                        \"SUSE (1994)\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Slackware, released July 1993, is the earliest listed distribution.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q05\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in September 1993?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Debian\",\n                        \"Fedora\",\n                        \"Ubuntu\",\n                        \"Mageia\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Debian was released in September 1993.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q06\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in May 1995?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Red Hat\",\n                        \"CentOS\",\n                        \"Knoppix\",\n                        \"Alpine Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Red Hat was released in May 1995.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q07\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in September 2000?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Knoppix\",\n                        \"Gentoo\",\n                        \"Arch Linux\",\n                        \"Musix\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Knoppix was released in September 2000.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q08\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which two distributions in the reference share the release date of March 2002?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Fedora and CentOS\",\n                        \"Gentoo and Arch Linux\",\n                        \"Ubuntu and Debian\",\n                        \"Oracle Linux and Musix\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Both Gentoo and Arch Linux were released in March 2002.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q09\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in November 2003?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Fedora\",\n                        \"Ubuntu\",\n                        \"CentOS\",\n                        \"Mageia\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Fedora was released in November 2003.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q10\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution is recorded in the reference only with the year 2004?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Ubuntu\",\n                        \"CentOS\",\n                        \"Gentoo\",\n                        \"Android\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"CentOS is recorded simply as 2004, while Ubuntu has October 2004.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q11\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in October 2004?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Ubuntu\",\n                        \"Alpine Linux\",\n                        \"Musix\",\n                        \"Oracle Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Ubuntu was released in October 2004.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q12\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in August 2005?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Alpine Linux\",\n                        \"Android\",\n                        \"Mageia\",\n                        \"Knoppix\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Alpine Linux was released in August 2005.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q13\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in October 2006?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Oracle Linux\",\n                        \"Musix\",\n                        \"Fedora\",\n                        \"CentOS\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Oracle Linux was released in October 2006.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q14\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in December 2006?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Musix\",\n                        \"Oracle Linux\",\n                        \"Ubuntu\",\n                        \"Arch Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Musix was released in December 2006.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q15\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in September 2008?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Android\",\n                        \"Mageia\",\n                        \"Alpine Linux\",\n                        \"SUSE\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Android was released in September 2008.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q16\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution in the reference has the most recent release date?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Mageia (June 2011)\",\n                        \"Android (September 2008)\",\n                        \"Musix (December 2006)\",\n                        \"Ubuntu (October 2004)\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Mageia, released June 2011, is the most recent listed distribution.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q17\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution was released in 1994?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"SUSE\",\n                        \"Red Hat\",\n                        \"Debian\",\n                        \"Fedora\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"SUSE was released in 1994.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q18\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these distributions appears in the reference timeline?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Manjaro\",\n                        \"Pop!_OS\",\n                        \"Slackware\",\n                        \"MX Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Slackware appears in the timeline, while the others are not listed in it.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q19\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does the abbreviation DVD stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Digital Versatile Disc\",\n                        \"Digital Visual Device\",\n                        \"Data Volume Drive\",\n                        \"Dynamic Video Disc\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"DVD stands for Digital Versatile Disc.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch3-q20\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which pairing of distribution and release year matches the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Fedora 2004\",\n                        \"Ubuntu 2004\",\n                        \"CentOS 2005\",\n                        \"Gentoo 2003\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Ubuntu was released in October 2004; Fedora was November 2003, CentOS 2004, and Gentoo March 2002.\"\n                }\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch4\",\n            \"title\": \"Desktop Environments, Package Management, and Boot Loaders\",\n            \"description\": \"Desktop environments, display renderers, display managers, partitioning utilities, Linux installation steps, package formats and package managers, system installers, and boot loaders with system requirements.\",\n            \"questions\": [\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q01\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does the abbreviation DE stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Display Environment\",\n                        \"Desktop Environment\",\n                        \"Device Emulator\",\n                        \"Data Encoder\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"DE stands for Desktop Environment.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q02\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does MBR stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Master Boot Record\",\n                        \"Main Boot Routine\",\n                        \"Master Backup Register\",\n                        \"Module Boot Resource\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"MBR stands for Master Boot Record.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q03\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does GPT stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Generic Partition Table\",\n                        \"Global Partition Type\",\n                        \"GUID Partition Table\",\n                        \"Grid Partition Tool\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"GPT is the GUID Partition Table.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q04\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does GUID stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Globally Unique Identifier\",\n                        \"General Unified Identifier\",\n                        \"Global User Index Data\",\n                        \"Generic Update ID\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"GUID stands for Globally Unique Identifier.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q05\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which two technologies are named as the major display renderers?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"GRUB and LILO\",\n                        \"Xorg / X Window System (X11) and Wayland\",\n                        \"GDM and LightDM\",\n                        \"fdisk and parted\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The major display renderers cited are Xorg / X Window System (X11) and Wayland.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q06\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a common Linux partitioning utility?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"GRUB\",\n                        \"LILO\",\n                        \"parted\",\n                        \"NTLDR\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"parted, fdisk, gdisk, cfdisk, gparted, and lsblk are the Linux partitioning utilities listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q07\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which display manager expands to GNOME Display Manager?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"GDM\",\n                        \"XDM\",\n                        \"KDM\",\n                        \"SDDM\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"GDM (or GDM3) is the GNOME Display Manager.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q08\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which display manager is the Simple Desktop Display Manager?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"LightDM\",\n                        \"SDDM\",\n                        \"LXDM\",\n                        \"CDM\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"SDDM stands for Simple Desktop Display Manager.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q09\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which display manager is described as Lightweight?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"LightDM\",\n                        \"KDM\",\n                        \"XDM\",\n                        \"WDM\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"LightDM is the Lightweight Display Manager; Ly is also described as Lightweight.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q10\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is listed as a Linux desktop environment?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"KDE Plasma\",\n                        \"AUTOEXEC.BAT\",\n                        \"BOOTMGR\",\n                        \"NTLDR\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"KDE Plasma, GNOME, XFCE, LXDE, and LXQt are among the desktop environments listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q11\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which sequence matches the Linux installation steps given in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Keyboard settings, Partition, Set Timezone, User Information, Install files\",\n                        \"Partition, Keyboard settings, User Information, Set Timezone, Install files\",\n                        \"Set Timezone, Keyboard settings, Partition, Install files, User Information\",\n                        \"User Information, Set Timezone, Keyboard settings, Partition, Install files\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The reference order is Keyboard settings, Partition, Set Timezone, User Information, then Install files.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q12\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package format is used by Gentoo Linux?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"RPM\",\n                        \"deb\",\n                        \"ebuild\",\n                        \"eopkg\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"ebuild is the Gentoo Linux package format.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q13\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package format is distribution-agnostic and mainly used by Ubuntu per the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Snap\",\n                        \"AppImage\",\n                        \"Flatpak\",\n                        \"MSI\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Snap is distribution-agnostic and mainly associated with Ubuntu.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q14\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package format is .pkg.tar.zst used by?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Arch Linux\",\n                        \"Solus\",\n                        \"Puppy Linux\",\n                        \"Alpine Linux\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \".pkg.tar.zst is the package format of Arch Linux.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q15\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package manager belongs to Node.js?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"pip\",\n                        \"npm\",\n                        \"vcpkg\",\n                        \"Homebrew\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"npm is the Node.js package manager.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q16\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package manager belongs to Python?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"pip\",\n                        \"nala\",\n                        \"pacman\",\n                        \"zypper\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"pip is the Python package manager.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q17\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package manager is used by Arch Linux?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"apt\",\n                        \"yum\",\n                        \"pacman\",\n                        \"dnf\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"pacman is the Arch Linux package manager.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q18\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which package manager is used by openSUSE and SUSE?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"zypper\",\n                        \"apt\",\n                        \"nala\",\n                        \"Homebrew\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"zypper is the package manager for openSUSE / SUSE.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q19\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is listed as an RPM mode?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Compile\",\n                        \"Sign\",\n                        \"Query\",\n                        \"Build\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"RPM modes include Query, Verify, Install, Upgrade, Erase, Show querytags, and Show configuration.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q20\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a Linux system installer?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Anaconda\",\n                        \"NTLDR\",\n                        \"BOOTMGR\",\n                        \"SYSLINUX\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Anaconda, Debian-Installer, DrakX, Preseed, Snap, Wubi, and Calamares are the system installers listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q21\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which task is performed by Debian-Installer?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Rendering the desktop\",\n                        \"Detecting the hardware\",\n                        \"Compiling the kernel\",\n                        \"Removing unused packages\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Debian-Installer detects hardware, installation media, and other OSes, configures the network and APT, and sets up user accounts.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q22\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a boot loader listed in the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"rEFInd\",\n                        \"GDM\",\n                        \"LXDM\",\n                        \"GUID\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"GNU GRUB, LILO, rEFInd, BootX, NTLDR, BURG, and SYSLINUX are the boot loaders listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q23\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which boot-related program is the Windows Boot Manager?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"NTLDR\",\n                        \"BootX\",\n                        \"BOOTMGR\",\n                        \"GRUB\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"BOOTMGR is the Windows Boot Manager; NTLDR is the NT Loader.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q24\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does GRUB stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"General Resource Utility Block\",\n                        \"GRand Unified Bootloader\",\n                        \"Global Run Uniform Boot\",\n                        \"Group Reader Update Block\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"GRUB is the GRand Unified Bootloader.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q25\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does UEFI stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Unified Extensible Firmware Interface\",\n                        \"Universal Extra Firmware Interface\",\n                        \"Unique Embedded File Index\",\n                        \"Unified External Format Interface\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"UEFI is the Unified Extensible Firmware Interface.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q26\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which distribution requires 8 GB RAM (12 GB recommended) per the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Ubuntu Linux\",\n                        \"Fedora Linux\",\n                        \"Kali Linux\",\n                        \"Linux Mint\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Fedora lists 8 GB RAM (12 GB recommended), Ubuntu and SUSE 4 GB, Linux Mint 4 GB+, and Kali 2 GB.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q27\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which desktop environment does the abbreviation XFCE stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"XFast Configuration Engine\",\n                        \"XForms Common Environment\",\n                        \"X File Control Extension\",\n                        \"XFrame Command Entry\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"XFCE stands for XForms Common Environment.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q28\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which window manager is the Binary Space Partitioning Window Manager?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"BSPWM\",\n                        \"LXQt\",\n                        \"LXDE\",\n                        \"UKUI\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"BSPWM is the Binary Space Partitioning Window Manager.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q29\",\n                    \"question\": \"What does XDMCP stand for?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"X Display Manager Control Protocol\",\n                        \"X Desktop Mode Configuration Pack\",\n                        \"X Domain Manager Control Program\",\n                        \"X Display Monitor Common Port\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"XDMCP is the X Display Manager Control Protocol.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch4-q30\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a package format consumed by Red Hat Enterprise Linux, Fedora, and CentOS?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"deb\",\n                        \"RPM\",\n                        \"APK\",\n                        \"ebuild\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"RPM is used by RHEL, Fedora, CentOS, SUSE Linux Enterprise Server, and openSUSE.\"\n                }\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n            \"title\": \"Basic Commands\",\n            \"description\": \"Linux paths, directory and file commands, more command navigation keys, user and group commands, /etc/passwd fields, UID ranges, filter commands, compression tools and tar options, utility commands, networking commands, and link commands.\",\n            \"questions\": [\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q01\",\n                    \"question\": \"What are the two types of Linux paths?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Primary and secondary paths\",\n                        \"Relative and absolute paths\",\n                        \"Local and remote paths\",\n                        \"Short and long paths\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Linux paths are either relative or absolute.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q02\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command prints the current working directory?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"pwd\",\n                        \"ls\",\n                        \"cd\",\n                        \"mkdir\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"pwd prints the working directory.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q03\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command makes a new directory?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"mkgrid\",\n                        \"mdir\",\n                        \"mkdir\",\n                        \"make-dir\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"mkdir (make directory) creates a new directory.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q04\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command removes an empty directory?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"rmdir\",\n                        \"del\",\n                        \"unrm\",\n                        \"rm-dir\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"rmdir (remove directory) removes an empty directory.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q05\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command creates empty files?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"touch\",\n                        \"cat\",\n                        \"echo\",\n                        \"head\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"touch creates empty files.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q06\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command concatenates and displays file content?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"cat\",\n                        \"tac\",\n                        \"sed\",\n                        \"vim\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"cat concatenates and displays file content.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q07\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command outputs a string?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"echo\",\n                        \"printf\",\n                        \"print\",\n                        \"say\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"echo outputs a string; printf is described as formatted printing.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q08\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command performs formatted printing according to the reference?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"echo\",\n                        \"print\",\n                        \"printf\",\n                        \"out\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"printf is the command for formatted printing.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q09\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command displays the first lines of a file?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"head\",\n                        \"tail\",\n                        \"top\",\n                        \"start\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"head displays the first lines of a file.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q10\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command displays file contents in reverse order?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"cat -r\",\n                        \"tac\",\n                        \"rev\",\n                        \"tail -a\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"tac displays file contents in reverse order.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q11\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command is described as an enhanced file viewer?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"less\",\n                        \"more\",\n                        \"view\",\n                        \"open\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"less is described as the enhanced file viewing command.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q12\",\n                    \"question\": \"In the more command, which key scrolls down page by line?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Space bar\",\n                        \"b\",\n                        \"Enter\",\n                        \"/\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"Enter scrolls down page by line in more.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q13\",\n                    \"question\": \"In the more command, which key moves to the previous page?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"b\",\n                        \"Space bar\",\n                        \"Enter\",\n                        \"/\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The b key moves to the previous page in more.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q14\",\n                    \"question\": \"In the more command, which key searches for a string?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"s\",\n                        \"f\",\n                        \"/\",\n                        \"q\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"The / key searches for a string in more.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q15\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command switches to the administrative user?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"su\",\n                        \"sudo\",\n                        \"id\",\n                        \"passwd\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"su switches to the administrative user.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q16\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command displays the UID and GID of a user?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"who\",\n                        \"id\",\n                        \"me\",\n                        \"info\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"id displays the UID and GID.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q17\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command adds a user account?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"useradd\",\n                        \"newuser\",\n                        \"mkuser\",\n                        \"add-account\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"useradd adds a user account.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q18\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command changes a user's password?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"passwd\",\n                        \"pwd\",\n                        \"chpass\",\n                        \"setpass\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"passwd changes the user password.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q19\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command deletes a user account?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"userdel\",\n                        \"userremove\",\n                        \"rmuser\",\n                        \"del-account\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"userdel (or deluser) deletes a user account.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q20\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command modifies an existing user account?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"useradd\",\n                        \"usermod\",\n                        \"chmod\",\n                        \"useredit\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"usermod modifies a user account.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q21\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command creates a user group?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"groupadd\",\n                        \"groupmod\",\n                        \"groupdel\",\n                        \"groupset\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"groupadd creates a user group.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q22\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is NOT a field in the /etc/passwd file?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"Username\",\n                        \"User ID (UID)\",\n                        \"Home directory\",\n                        \"Default gateway\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n                    \"explanation\": \"/etc/passwd fields are username, password marker, UID, GID, GECOS info, home directory, and shell; the gateway is not among them.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q23\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which UID is reserved for the root user?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"0\",\n                        \"1\",\n                        \"100\",\n                        \"1000\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"UID 0 is reserved for the root user.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q24\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which UID range is reserved for regular user accounts?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"1-99\",\n                        \"100-999\",\n                        \"1000 and above\",\n                        \"5000 and above\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n                    \"explanation\": \"UID 1000 and above is for regular user accounts.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q25\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which UID range covers predefined system accounts?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"1-99\",\n                        \"100-999\",\n                        \"1000+\",\n                        \"0-10\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"UID 1-99 is for predefined system accounts; 100-999 covers system accounts and groups.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q26\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which of these is a Linux filter command?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"grep\",\n                        \"cp\",\n                        \"ls\",\n                        \"cd\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"grep, wc, od, and sort are the filter commands listed.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q27\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which tar option creates an archive?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"-c\",\n                        \"-x\",\n                        \"-t\",\n                        \"-z\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"-c creates an archive, -x extracts, -t views content, and -z filters through gzip.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q28\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which tar option filters the archive through bzip2?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"-z\",\n                        \"-j\",\n                        \"-v\",\n                        \"-W\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n                    \"explanation\": \"-j filters through bzip2 while -z filters through gzip.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q29\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command performs DNS lookups?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"host\",\n                        \"ping\",\n                        \"ip\",\n                        \"mail\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"host performs DNS lookups; ping tests connectivity using ICMP.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"id\": \"ch5-q30\",\n                    \"question\": \"Which command creates a symbolic (soft) link?\",\n                    \"choices\": [\n                        \"ln -s\",\n                        \"link\",\n                        \"cp -l\",\n                        \"mv -l\"\n                    ],\n                    \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n                    \"explanation\": \"ln -s creates a symbolic/soft link, while link or ln creates a hard link.\"\n                }\n            ]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"encyclopedia\": [\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"term\": \"Kernel\",\n            \"definition\": \"The core of the operating system that establishes communication between user-level applications and hardware, and manages CPU/GPU, memory, I/O devices, resources, devices, system calls, and security and protection.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"term\": \"LAMP\",\n            \"definition\": \"A software stack abbreviation for Linux, Apache, MySQL, and Perl/PHP/Python.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"term\": \"GNU\",\n            \"definition\": \"A recursive acronym for GNU's Not Unix, supported by the Free Software Foundation (FSF).\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"term\": \"Monolithic kernel\",\n            \"definition\": \"One of the kernel types listed in the reference, together with microkernel, hybrid, exo kernel, nano kernel, VM kernel or hypervisor, and embedded kernel.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"term\": \"bash\",\n            \"definition\": \"The GNU Bourne-Again Shell; its root prompt is bash-VersionNumber# and its non-root prompt is bash-VersionNumber$.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n            \"term\": \"Inter-Process Communication\",\n            \"definition\": \"A kernel function that handles communication between running processes.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n            \"term\": \"Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS)\",\n            \"definition\": \"The standard that defines the Linux directory structure, such as /etc for configuration, /home for user homes, /dev for device files, /proc for process and kernel information, and /var for variable data.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n            \"term\": \"ZFS\",\n            \"definition\": \"The Zettabyte File System; common use cases include Enterprise Storage Solutions, Backup and Disaster Recovery, and File Server and NAS.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n            \"term\": \"NFS\",\n            \"definition\": \"Network File System, composed of an NFS Server and an NFS Client.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n            \"term\": \"Btrfs\",\n            \"definition\": \"The B-tree File System, listed among the Linux file systems alongside Minix, the ext family, ReiserFS, XFS, ZFS, and JFS.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n            \"term\": \"ReFS\",\n            \"definition\": \"The Resilient File System, a Microsoft Windows file system.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch3\",\n            \"term\": \"Slackware\",\n            \"definition\": \"The earliest distribution in the reference timeline, released in July 1993.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch3\",\n            \"term\": \"Mageia\",\n            \"definition\": \"The most recent distribution in the reference timeline, released in June 2011.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch4\",\n            \"term\": \"Display manager\",\n            \"definition\": \"Software that provides a graphical login; examples include GDM, SDDM, LightDM, LXDM, XDM, Ly, KDM, WDM, MDM, CDM, SLiM, Entrance, XDMCP, emptty, and greetd.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch4\",\n            \"term\": \"RPM\",\n            \"definition\": \"The RPM Package Manager (originally Red Hat Package Manager), used by Red Hat Enterprise Linux, Fedora, CentOS, SUSE Linux Enterprise Server, and openSUSE.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch4\",\n            \"term\": \"GRUB\",\n            \"definition\": \"The GRand Unified Bootloader, one of the boot loaders listed in the reference along with LILO, rEFInd, BootX, NTLDR, BURG, and SYSLINUX.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch4\",\n            \"term\": \"UEFI\",\n            \"definition\": \"The Unified Extensible Firmware Interface, which replaces the traditional BIOS-based boot environment.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n            \"term\": \"pwd\",\n            \"definition\": \"The command that prints the working directory; a Linux directory command along with mkdir, rmdir, ls, and cd.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n            \"term\": \"/etc/passwd\",\n            \"definition\": \"A file whose fields are the username, password (x marker), UID, GID, GECOS or user info, home directory, and shell.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n            \"term\": \"tar options\",\n            \"definition\": \"Options such as -c (create), -x (extract), -v (verbose), -f (filename), -t (view), -j (bzip2), -z (gzip), -r (append), -W (verify), and -C (extract to a different directory).\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n            \"term\": \"UID ranges\",\n            \"definition\": \"UID 0 is the root user, UID 1-99 are predefined system accounts, UID 100-999 are system accounts and groups, and UID 1000 and above are regular user accounts.\"\n        }\n    ]\n}"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\29l6wh2f5izows\\questions.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\29l6wh2f5izows\\questions.json",